== Permutation Representations and Burnside Rings
<sec:permutation-representations-burnside-rings>

The concept of a finite group $G$ acting as a group of permutations on a finite
set $S$ arises naturally in many applications of group theory. We have already
met this idea in §§~@i:sec:background-algebras-groups, @i:sec:induced-modules,
and @i:sec:clifford-theory-hecke-algebras. Of course, a permutation
representation of $G$ can be described by permutation matrices with entries in a
field or a commutative ring. However, permutation representations have various
formal properties that are independent of the choice of ground ring. These
properties may be viewed as the framework of a number of standard constructions
in representation theory over fields or rings. This connection will be made
explicit in the discussion to follow, when we develop the formal properties of
permutation representations.

As in §~@i:sec:background-algebras-groups, a _$G$-set_ is a finite set $S$ on
which a finite group $G$ acts (from the left) as a group of permutations. In
studying $G$-sets for a given group $G$, it is convenient to introduce the
_Burnside ring_ $Omega(G)$ consisting of formal sums and differences of
$G$-sets. This procedure is analogous to the step from characters of $G$ to the
character ring $op("ch")_F G$ over some field $F$.
#idx("G-set")

Throughout this section, $G$ denotes a finite group, and $H, K$ denote subgroups
of $G$. For a pair of $G$-sets $S$ and $T$, we let $S union.dot T$ be their
disjoint union, and $S times T$ their cartesian product.

=== Burnside Rings <ss:burnside-rings>

Throughout, let $G$ be a finite group. After defining various operations on
$G$-sets, we shall introduce the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$ consisting of formal
$ZZ$-linear combinations of $G$-sets. We shall discuss the structure of the ring
$Omega(G)$. The results in this subsection are due mainly to Burnside, A. Dress,
and L. Solomon, and our treatment follows the approach in
Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a and Dress~@bib:Dress1971a.

A (left) _$G$-set_ is a finite set $S$ on which $G$ acts from the left as a
group of permutations (see @i:def:g-set–@i:exm:group-actions). For $H <= G$, the
collection $G slash H$ of left cosets ${y H}$ of $H$ in $G$ is a transitive
$G$-set, and indeed every transitive $G$-set is isomorphic to $G slash H$ for
some $H$ (see @i:prop:orbits-stabilizers). Let $e_G$ denote the $G$-set
consisting of a single element, also denoted by $e_G$, with $x e_G = e_G$ for
all $x in G$. Clearly $e_G tilde.equiv G slash G$ as $G$-sets.

Given arbitrary $G$-sets $S$ and $T$, we may form their disjoint union
$S union.dot T$ and their cartesian product $S times T$, both of which are
$G$-sets. The action of $G$ on
#source(855)
$S times T$ is defined by
$ x(s, t) = (x s, x t) quad "for" x in G, s in S, t in T. $
View $S union.dot T$ as the “sum,” and $S times T$ as the “product,” of the
$G$-sets $S$ and $T$.

By definition, a _$G$-map_ $f: S -> T$ is a set map such that
$ f(x s) = x f(s) quad "for all" x in G, s in S. $
Let $Hom_G lr((S, T))$ denote the collection of all such maps $f: S -> T$. Note
that $Hom_G lr((S, T))$ is not in general a $G$-set. We write $S tilde.equiv T$
if there is a $G$-bijection from $S$ onto $T$.

For each element $s$ in a $G$-set $S$, its _orbit_ $G s = {x s: x in G}$ is the
smallest $G$-set containing $s$. Then $S$ is uniquely expressible as a disjoint
union of its distinct $G$-orbits. For $s in S$, its _stabilizer_ is defined by
$ G_s = {x in G: x s = s} <= G. $
#idx("Stabilizer")
By @i:prop:orbits-stabilizers, the orbit of $s$ is isomorphic to the left coset
space $G slash G_s$ as $G$-sets.

By definition, a _simple $G$-set_ is a nonempty $G$-set with no proper
$G$-subsets. It must consist of a single orbit, and hence is a left coset space
$G slash H$ for some $H <= G$. Conversely, each $G slash H$ is simple.

#definition[
  The _Burnside ring_ $Omega(G)$ of a finite group $G$ is the abelian group
  generated by symbols $[S]$, one for each isomorphism class of finite $G$-sets
  $S$, with relations
  $ [S union.dot T] = [S] + [T] quad "for" S, T space G"-sets". $
  <eq:burnside-disjoint-union>
  Multiplication is defined by
  $ [S][T] = [S times T] quad "for" S, T space G"-sets", $
  <eq:burnside-cartesian-product>
  extended to $Omega(G)$ by linearity. Then $Omega(G)$ is a commutative ring
  with identity element $[e_G]$.
] <def:burnside-ring>
#idx("Burnside ring", target: [@def:burnside-ring])
#nidx("$Omega(G)$", "Burnside ring", target: [@def:burnside-ring])

To be more precise, $Omega(G)$ is defined in a manner similar to
@i:def:grothendieck-category-group, where we introduced the Grothendieck group
of a category of modules. In the present case, we define $Omega(G) = F
slash F_0$, where $F$ is the free abelian group generated by symbols $(S)$, one
for each isomorphism class of $G$-sets $S$, and where $F_0$ is the subgroup of
$F$ generated by all expressions $(S union.dot T) - (S) - (T)$. Then $Omega(G)$
is an abelian additive group. Defining multiplication as in
@eq:burnside-cartesian-product on the generators of $F$, we see easily that
$F_0$ is an ideal of $F$, and therefore $Omega(G)$ has a well-defined ring
structure. Since $S times T tilde.equiv T times S$ for any $G$-sets $S$ and $T$,
it is clear that the ring $Omega(G)$ is commutative. Furthermore,
$e_G times T tilde.equiv T$ for each $G$-set $T$, so $[e_G]$ is the identity
element of $Omega(G)$.

#source(856)
We prove at once:

#lemma[
  Let $S, T$ be $G$-sets. Then $[S] = [T]$ in $Omega(G)$ if and only if
  $S tilde.equiv T$ as $G$-sets.
] <lem:burnside-g-set-cancellation>
#proof[
  If $[S] = [T]$ in $Omega(G)$, then $S union.dot X tilde.equiv T union.dot X$
  for some $G$-set $X$ (see proof of
  @prop:grothendieck-equal-classes-criterion). We must show that
  $S tilde.equiv T$. Let ${S_1, dots, S_k}$ be a full set of nonisomorphic
  simple $G$-sets. From the orbit decomposition of $S$, it follows that
  $ S tilde.equiv S_1^((n_1)) union.dot dots union.dot S_k^((n_k)), $
  where $S_i^((n_i))$ denotes the disjoint union of $n_i$ copies of $S_i$. The
  isomorphism class of $S$ uniquely determines the $k$-tuple $(n_1, dots, n_k)$.
  Therefore, if $S union.dot X tilde.equiv T union.dot X$, then $S$ and $T$ must
  have the same orbit structure, so $S tilde.equiv T$, as claimed. Conversely,
  $S tilde.equiv T$ implies $[S] = [T]$, and the lemma is proved.
]
(We have in fact shown that $[S_1], dots, [S_k]$ is a free $ZZ$-basis of
$Omega(G)$; compare the above argument with the proof of
@i:prop:artinian-grothendieck-simple-basis.)

Some notation will be needed for the discussion to follow. Let $H, K$ denote
subgroups of the finite group $G$. As in §~@i:sec:background-algebras-groups,
call $H$ and $K$ _$G$-conjugate_ (notation: $H =_G K$) if $x^(-1) H x = K$ for
some $x in G$. Also, if $x^(-1) H x subset.eq K$ for some $x in G$, we write
$H <=_G K$, and say that $H$ is _subconjugate_ to $K$.
#idx("Subconjugate")

Now let $cal(S) = cal(S)(G)$ be a full set of nonconjugate subgroups of $G$. We
intend to show that the $G$-sets ${G slash H: H in cal(S)}$ form a $ZZ$-basis of
$Omega(G)$, and the only difficulty in proving this lies in showing that if
$G slash H tilde.equiv G slash K$, then necessarily $H =_G K$. For this purpose,
we need the concept (already encountered in §~@i:ss:outer-tensor-products) of
the _$G$-invariant subset_ $op("inv")_G lr((S))$ of a $G$-set $S$. We define
$ op("inv")_G lr((S)) = {s in S: x s = s "for all" x in G}. $
We now establish:

#proposition[
  Let $H$ and $K$ be subgroups of $G$, and let $S$ be any $G$-set. Then
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        There is a bijection
        $ Hom_G lr((G slash H, S)) arrow.l.r op("inv")_H lr((S)). $
      ] <case:g-set-hom-fixed-points>],
    [
      #case[
        There is a bijection
        $
          Hom_G lr((G slash H, G slash K)) arrow.l.r op("inv")_H lr(
            (G
              slash K)
          ).
        $
        Further, $op("inv")_H lr((G slash K)) = {x K: x in G, x^(-1) H x <= K}$.
      ] <case:g-coset-hom-fixed-points>],
    [
      #case[
        $op("inv")_H lr((G slash K)) = emptyset$ unless $H <=_G K$.
      ] <case:g-coset-fixed-points-subconjugacy>],
    [
      #case[
        $G slash H tilde.equiv G slash K$ as $G$-sets if and only if $H =_G K$.
      ] <case:g-coset-isomorphism-conjugacy>],
  )
] <prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points>
#source(857)
#proof[
  @case:g-set-hom-fixed-points Each $f in Hom_G lr((G slash H, S))$ maps the
  $H$-trivial element $1 dot H in G slash H$ onto an $H$-trivial element
  $s_0 in S$. Further, $f$ is completely determined by $s_0$, since
  $f(x H) = x s_0$ for all $x in G$. The correspondence $f arrow.l.r s_0$ gives
  the desired bijection.

  @case:g-coset-hom-fixed-points Taking $S = G slash K$ in
  @case:g-set-hom-fixed-points, we note that the left coset $x K$ is
  $H$-invariant if and only if $H x K = x K$, that is, $x^(-1) H x <= K$. This
  establishes @case:g-coset-hom-fixed-points, as well as
  @case:g-coset-fixed-points-subconjugacy.

  @case:g-coset-isomorphism-conjugacy If $H =_G K$, write $K = x H x^(-1)$ with
  $x in G$. Then there is a $G$-isomorphism $theta: G slash K tilde.equiv G
  slash H$, given by
  $ theta(g K) = theta(g x H x^(-1)) = g x H, quad "for" g in G. $
  Conversely, suppose that $G slash H tilde.equiv G slash K$. Then
  $Hom_G lr((G slash H, G slash K)) != emptyset$, so $H <=_G K$ by
  @case:g-coset-fixed-points-subconjugacy. Therefore $H =_G K$ by symmetry, and
  the proposition is proved.
]

As an immediate consequence of @case:g-coset-isomorphism-conjugacy[#context {
  reference-number-text(
    "("
      + reference-data(<prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points>).text
      + reference-data(<case:g-coset-isomorphism-conjugacy>)
        .text
        .trim(regex("[()]"))
      + ")",
  )
}] and our earlier remarks, we have:

#corollary[
  Let $cal(S)$ be a full set of nonconjugate subgroups of $G$. Then
  $ Omega(G) = plus.o.big_(H in cal(S)) ZZ[G slash H]. $
] <cor:burnside-transitive-basis>

Now let $H$ be a subgroup of $G$. If $S$ and $T$ are $G$-sets, it is clear that
$
  op("inv")_H lr((S union.dot T))
  &= op("inv")_H lr((S)) union.dot op("inv")_H lr((T)), \
  op("inv")_H lr((S times T))
  &= op("inv")_H lr((S)) times op("inv")_H lr((T)).
$
These formulas suggest that we introduce a map
$
  phi_H: Omega(G) -> ZZ, quad "defined by"
  phi_(H)[S] = abs(op("inv")_H lr((S))), quad S = G"-set".
$ <eq:burnside-fixed-point-mark>
It is easily seen that $phi_H$ is well defined. The preceding formulas show
that, in fact, the map $phi_H$ is a ring homomorphism.

If $H$ and $K$ are $G$-conjugate, it is easily shown that $phi_H = phi_K$. In
fact, for any $x in G$ and any $G$-set $S$, we have
$
  s in op("inv")_H lr((S))
  <=> x s in op("inv")_(x H x^(-1)) lr((S)).
$
Therefore $phi_H = phi_K$ whenever $H =_G K$. As we shall see below in
@eq:burnside-domain-homomorphism-mark, the converse also holds.

Let $H <= G$; using @case:g-coset-hom-fixed-points[#context {
  reference-number-text(
    "("
      + reference-data(<prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points>).text
      + reference-data(<case:g-coset-hom-fixed-points>).text.trim(regex("[()]"))
      + ")",
  )
}] with $K = H$, we have
$ phi_(H)[G slash H] = abs(N_G lr((H)): H), $
<eq:burnside-diagonal-mark>
where $N_G lr((H))$ is the normalizer of $H$ in $G$. Further,
@case:g-coset-fixed-points-subconjugacy[#context {
  reference-number-text(
    "("
      + reference-data(<prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points>).text
      + reference-data(<case:g-coset-fixed-points-subconjugacy>)
        .text
        .trim(regex("[()]"))
      + ")",
  )
}] gives
$ phi_(H)[G slash K] != 0 <=> H <=_G K. $
<eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy>

#source(858)
We shall use the above formulas to prove that the collection of ring
homomorphisms ${phi_H: H in cal(S)(G)}$, from $Omega(G)$ to $ZZ$, suffice to
distinguish the elements of the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$ from one another. To be
explicit, we shall establish:

#theorem(title: [Burnside])[
  Let $S$ and $T$ be $G$-sets, and let $cal(S)$ be a full set of nonconjugate
  subgroups of $G$. Then $S tilde.equiv T$ if and only if
  $phi_(H)[S] = phi_(H)[T]$ for all $H in cal(S)$.
] <th:burnside-fixed-point-isomorphism>
#idx("Burnside's Theorem", target: [@th:burnside-fixed-point-isomorphism])
#proof[
  The result is obvious in one direction, so now assume that
  $phi_(H)[S] = phi_(H)[T]$ for all $H in cal(S)$, and let us prove that
  $S tilde.equiv T$. We may write
  $
    [S] = sum m_(i)[G slash H_i], quad [T] = sum n_(i)[G slash H_i], quad m_i,
    n_i in ZZ,
  $
  where $H_i$ ranges over all elements of $cal(S)$. We must prove that
  $m_i = n_i$ for each $i$. Suppose this is false, and let
  $ cal(S)_0 = {H_i in cal(S): m_i != n_i}. $
  Partially order $cal(S)$ with respect to $<=_G$, and let $H_0$ be a maximal
  element of $cal(S)_0$. By @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy, #named-equation(
    name: [$(ast)$],
  )[
    $
      phi_(H_0)[G slash H] = 0 quad
      "for all" H in cal(S)_0 "different from" H_0.
    $
  ] <eq:burnside-maximal-support-mark>
  Since $phi_(H_0)[S] = phi_(H_0)[T]$ by hypothesis, and since $m_i = n_i$
  whenever $H_i in.not cal(S)_0$, we obtain
  $
    sum_(H_i in cal(S)_0) m_i phi_(H_0)[G slash H_i]
    = sum_(H_i in cal(S)_0) n_i phi_(H_0)[G slash H_i].
  $
  Using @eq:burnside-maximal-support-mark above, it follows that
  $m_0 phi_(H_0)[G slash H_0] = n_0 phi_(H_0)[G slash H_0]$, and therefore
  $m_0 = n_0$
  because $phi_(H_0)[G slash H_0] != 0$ by @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy. But
  $m_0 != n_0$ because $H_0 in cal(S)_0$, so we have obtained a contradiction.
  Thus $cal(S)_0$ is empty, and $m_i = n_i$ for each $H_i in cal(S)$. Therefore
  $S tilde.equiv T$ as claimed, and the proof is complete.
]

We shall now combine all of the ring homomorphisms ${phi_H: H in cal(S)}$ into a
single homomorphism $phi$. Let
$ cal(S)(G) = {H_1, dots, H_m}, $
and define
$
  phi: Omega(G) -> ZZ^((m)) quad "by"
  phi[S] = (phi_(H_1)[S], dots, phi_(H_m)[S]), quad S = G"-set".
$ <eq:burnside-total-mark-homomorphism>
Then $phi$ is a ring homomorphism, and $phi[e_G]$ is the identity element of the
ring $ZZ^((m))$. We now prove:

#source(859)
#proposition[
  The map $phi: Omega(G) -> ZZ^((m))$, defined above, is a ring monomorphism.
  Its image is a full#footnote[
    If $A$ is a free $ZZ$-module on $m$ generators, a _full $ZZ$-lattice_ in $A$
    is a $ZZ$-submodule that also has $m$ free generators.
  ] $ZZ$-lattice in $ZZ^((m))$. Further, the $m$ functions
  ${phi_(H_i): 1 <= i <= m}$ are linearly independent over $ZZ$.
] <prop:burnside-mark-full-lattice>
#proof[
  To show that $phi$ is a monomorphism, suppose that $S$ and $T$ are $G$-sets,
  and that $phi[S] = phi[T]$. Then $phi_(H)[S] = phi_(H)[T]$ for all
  $H in cal(S)$, and therefore $S tilde.equiv T$ by Burnside's Theorem
  @th:burnside-fixed-point-isomorphism. This proves that $phi$ is a
  monomorphism. Since $Omega(G)$ is $ZZ$-free of rank $m$, so is $im(phi)$, and
  thus $im(phi)$ is a full $ZZ$-sublattice of $ZZ^((m))$. Finally, the maps
  ${phi_H: H in cal(S)}$ must be linearly independent over $ZZ$, since otherwise
  $im(phi)$ cannot have $ZZ$-rank $m$.
]

For the above-defined $phi: Omega(G) -> ZZ^((m))$, the cokernel of $phi$ is a
$ZZ$-torsion module. Therefore $phi$ induces an isomorphism of $QQ$-algebras
$ QQ times.o_ZZ Omega(G) tilde.equiv QQ^((m)). $
<eq:rational-burnside-mark-isomorphism>
This result was first proved by Solomon~@bib:Solomon1967; it shows in particular
that $QQ times.o_ZZ Omega(G)$ is a semisimple $QQ$-algebra.

For $H <= G$, we set
$
  H^ast = product.co_(K in cal(S)(G))
  (phi_(K)[G slash H]) / (phi_(H)[G slash H]) in ZZ^((m)).
$ <eq:burnside-normalized-mark-vector>
(By Exercise @exc:normalizer-divides-fixed-point-marks, $H^ast$ is indeed an
element of $ZZ^((m))$.) We prove next:

#proposition[
  The elements ${H^ast: H in cal(S)(G)}$ form a free $ZZ$-basis of $ZZ^((m))$.
] <prop:burnside-normalized-mark-basis>
#proof[
  Let $Omega^ast = sum_(H in cal(S)) ZZ dot H^ast$. Since there are $m$ elements
  $H^ast$, we need only show that $Omega^ast = ZZ^((m))$. Let
  ${delta_1, dots, delta_m}$ be a standard basis of $ZZ^((m))$, so $delta_i$ is
  the $m$-tuple with $1$ at position $i$ and zeros elsewhere. If
  $Omega^ast ⊊ ZZ^((m))$, let $H_r in cal(S)$ be a minimal element of $cal(S)$
  such that $delta_r in.not Omega^ast$; here, we view $cal(S)$ as partially
  ordered with respect to $<=_G$, as usual. Then $delta_i in Omega^ast$ for all
  $H_i in (H_r)^-$, where $(H_r)^-$ consists of all $H in cal(S)$ that are
  conjugate to proper subgroups of $H_r$.

  By @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy, $H^ast$ has nonzero $K$-th component if and
  only if $K <=_G H$, and $H^ast$ obviously has $H$-th component $1$. Therefore
  $
    H_r^ast = delta_r + sum_(H_i in H_r^-) alpha_i delta_i,
    quad alpha_i in ZZ.
  $
  But each $delta_i$ occurring in the summation lies in $Omega^ast$, and so does
  $H_r^ast$. Therefore also $delta_r in Omega^ast$, which is a contradiction.
  This completes the proof.
]

#corollary[
  Let $n in ZZ$. Then $n dot ZZ^((m)) subset.eq phi{Omega(G)}$ if and only if
  $n equiv 0 (mod abs(G))$.
] <cor:burnside-mark-cokernel-exponent>
#source(860)
#proof[
  We have
  $
    ZZ^((m)) = plus.o.big_(H in cal(S)) ZZ H^ast, quad
    phi{Omega(G)} = plus.o.big_(H in cal(S)) ZZ dot phi[G slash H],
  $
  while for each $H <= G$,
  $ phi[G slash H] = phi_(H)[G slash H] dot H^ast. $
  Therefore $n dot op("coker") phi = 0$ if and only if $n$ is a multiple of
  $phi_(H)[G slash H]$ for each $H in cal(S)$. But $phi_(H)[G slash H] =
  abs(N_G lr((H)): H)$, which equals $abs(G)$ for $H = 1$, and otherwise is a
  divisor of $abs(G)$. This implies the result.
]

The above techniques allow one to find a full set of primitive orthogonal
idempotents in $QQ times.o_ZZ Omega(G)$; for details, see Gluck~@bib:Gluck1981.

We turn next to the question of finding all prime ideals of $Omega(G)$, and
begin with an analogue of Mackey's Tensor Product Theorem for $G$-sets:

#proposition[
  Let $H <= G$, and let $S$ be any $G$-set. Define
  $ H^- = {K in cal(S)(G): K <_G H}, $
  so $H^-$ does not include the subgroup $H$ itself. Then in $Omega(G)$ we have
  #equation-suffix(base: [@prop:burnside-transitive-product-triangular])[
    $
      [G slash H][S] = phi_(H)[S] dot [G slash H]
      + sum_(K in H^-) n_(K)[G slash K]
    $
  ] <eq:burnside-transitive-product-triangular>
  for some nonnegative integers ${n_K}$.
] <prop:burnside-transitive-product-triangular>
#proof[
  By definition of multiplication in the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$, we have
  #named-equation(name: [$(ast)$])[
    $
      [G slash H][S] = [(G slash H) times S]
      = sum_(K in cal(S)) n_(K)[G slash K]
    $
  ] <eq:burnside-product-orbit-expansion>
  for some nonnegative integers ${n_K}$. For fixed $K_0 in cal(S)$, we apply
  $phi_(K_0)$ to both sides of the preceding equation, obtaining
  $
    phi_(K_0)[G slash H] dot phi_(K_0)[S]
    = sum_K n_K phi_(K_0)[G slash K] >= 0.
  $
  Strict inequality holds whenever $n_(K_0) > 0$, and in that case we must have
  $phi_(K_0)[G slash H] != 0$; but by @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy, this
  implies that either $K_0 = H$ or else $K_0 in H^-$. Equation
  @eq:burnside-product-orbit-expansion now becomes
  $ [G slash H][S] = n_(H)[G slash H] + sum_(K in H^-) n_(K)[G slash K], $
  and it remains for us to calculate the integer $n_H$. Apply $phi_H$ to the
  above equation;
  #source(861)
  by @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy, $phi_(H)[G slash K] = 0$ for each
  $K in H^-$, so we get
  $ phi_(H)[G slash H] phi_(H)[S] = n_H phi_(H)[G slash H]. $
  Since $phi_(H)[G slash H] != 0$ by @eq:burnside-diagonal-mark, we have
  $n_H = phi_(H)[S]$, which completes the proof.
]

#corollary[
  Let $R$ be an integral domain, and let $theta: Omega(G) -> R$ be a ring
  homomorphism. Then there exists a subgroup $H$ of $G$ such that
  $ theta[S] = phi_(H)[S] dot 1_R quad "for each" G"-set" S. $
  <eq:burnside-domain-homomorphism-mark>
] <cor:burnside-domain-homomorphisms>
#proof[
  Partially order $cal(S)$ with respect to $<=_G$, and let $H in cal(S)$ be
  minimal such that $theta[G slash H] != 0$; then $theta[G slash K] = 0$ for
  $K in H^-$. Applying $theta$ to both sides of
  @eq:burnside-transitive-product-triangular, we obtain
  @eq:burnside-domain-homomorphism-mark at once.
]

If $R$ is an integral domain of characteristic zero, and if $H$ and $H'$ are
subgroups of $G$ giving rise to a pair of ring homomorphisms $theta$ and
$theta'$ from $Omega(G)$ into $R$, then we may conclude from Burnside's Theorem
@th:burnside-fixed-point-isomorphism that $theta = theta'$ if and only if $H$ is
$G$-conjugate to $H'$. The situation changes drastically when $R$ is a domain of
characteristic $p$, where $p$ is a positive prime.

In the discussion below, $p$ denotes either a rational prime number or zero. The
prime ideals of the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$ are precisely the kernels of ring
homomorphisms from $Omega(G)$ into an integral domain. Each ring homomorphism is
given by a map $theta$ as in @eq:burnside-domain-homomorphism-mark, for some
$H <= G$. This suggests that for each $H <= G$ and each $p$, we should consider
the ideal
$ I(H, p) = {x in Omega(G): phi_H lr((x)) equiv 0 (mod p)}. $
<eq:burnside-prime-mark-ideal>
Each such $I(H, p)$ is then necessarily a prime ideal of $Omega(G)$, since
obviously $I(H, p)$ is a proper ideal of $Omega(G)$ because
$[e_G] in.not I(H, p)$. We now prove:

#proposition(title: [Dress])[
  The prime ideals of the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$ are precisely the ideals
  $I(H, p)$ defined in @eq:burnside-prime-mark-ideal, where $p$ is either a
  rational prime or else $p = 0$.
] <prop:burnside-prime-ideal-classification>
#proof[
  By virtue of the preceding discussion, we need only show that every prime
  ideal $P$ of $Omega(G)$ is of the form $I(H, p)$ for some $H$ and $p$. But the
  natural map $Omega(G) -> Omega(G) slash P = R$ is a ring homomorphism, and
  hence is of the form @eq:burnside-domain-homomorphism-mark for some $H <= G$.
  This map carries $x in Omega(G)$ onto $phi_H lr((x)) dot 1_R$. If $R$ has
  characteristic $p$, the kernel of the map is precisely $I(H, p)$. Therefore
  $P = I(H, p)$, as claimed, and the result is proved.
]

Following Dress~@bib:Dress1971a, we now consider the question as to when two
prime ideals $I(H, p)$ and $I(K, q)$ of $Omega(G)$ coincide. Since $p$ is the
characteristic of the factor ring $Omega(G) slash I(H, p)$, it follows that
necessarily $p = q$. We must therefore decide
#source(862)
under which conditions it happens that $I(H, p) = I(K, p)$. The case $p = 0$ is
straightforward:

#proposition[
  The prime ideals $I(H, 0)$ and $I(K, 0)$ of $Omega(G)$ coincide if and only if
  $H =_G K$, or equivalently, $phi_H = phi_K$.
] <prop:burnside-characteristic-zero-prime-uniqueness>
#proof[
  We have already seen that
  $ H =_G K => phi_H = phi_K => I(H, 0) = I(K, 0). $
  Conversely, suppose that $I(H, 0) = I(K, 0)$. Then for $x in Omega(G)$,
  $ phi_H lr((x)) = 0 <=> phi_K lr((x)) = 0. $
  But $phi_(H)[G slash H] != 0$ by @eq:burnside-diagonal-mark, so $phi_(K)[G
    slash H] != 0$, and therefore $K <=_G H$ by @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy.
  By symmetry, we obtain $H <=_G K$, so $H$ and $K$ must be $G$-conjugate. This
  completes the proof.
]

Now let $p > 0$, and denote by $H^((p))$ the smallest normal subgroup of $H$
whose index is a power of $p$. Then $H^((p))$ is a uniquely determined
characteristic subgroup of $H$. We now state without proof (see
Dress~@bib:Dress1971a and Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a):

#theorem(name: "Dress's Theorem")[
  Let $p > 0$ be prime. The following are equivalent:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        $phi_H equiv phi_K (mod p)$, that is,
        $phi_(H)[S] equiv phi_(K)[S] (mod p)$ for each $G$-set $S$.
      ] <case:burnside-modular-marks-equal>],
    [
      #case[$I(H, p) = I(K, p)$.] <case:burnside-prime-mark-ideals-equal>],
    [
      #case[$H^((p))$ is $G$-conjugate to $K^((p))$.]
      <case:burnside-p-residuals-conjugate>],
  )
] <th:dress-burnside-prime-ideal-equality>
#idx("Dress' theorem", target: [@th:dress-burnside-prime-ideal-equality])

*Notes*

#case-list(
  numbering: "(i)",
  [
    #case[
      Using @th:dress-burnside-prime-ideal-equality, Dress showed that the prime
      spectrum of the ring $Omega(G)$ is connected if and only if $G$ is
      solvable. This leads to the tantalizing result: $G$ is solvable and of odd
      order if and only if the only units in $Omega(G)$ are $plus.minus 1$. For
      details, see Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a.
    ] <case:burnside-connected-spectrum-solvability>],
  [
    #case[
      For further results on Burnside rings, see Gustafson~@bib:Gustafson1977,
      Li~@bib:Li1974, Gluck~@bib:Gluck1981, Matsuda~@bib:Matsuda1982, and
      Yoshida~@bib:Yoshida1983.
    ] <case:burnside-further-results>],
)

=== G-Sets and Induction Maps <ss:g-sets-induction-maps>

Keeping the notation of §~@ss:burnside-rings, let $G$ be a finite group and $S$
a left $G$-set. Given a commutative ring $R$, let $R[S]$ be the _permutation
module_ having as $R$-basis the elements of $S$, on which $G$ acts as
permutation group. We may view $R[S]$ as a left $R G$-module, in an obvious way.
#idx("Permutation module")
There is a close analogy between the theory of permutation modules and the
theory of $G$-sets. Certain standard concepts, such as restriction, induction,
the Mackey theorems, and Frobenius
#source(863)
reciprocity, apply equally well to modules and to $G$-sets. In a sense, the
theory of $G$-sets is more fundamental, and underlies many proofs that depend on
manipulations in the lattice of subgroups of $G$. For example, the Burnside ring
$Omega(G)$ is the basic instance of a Frobenius functor, and character rings,
Grothendieck groups, etc. are modules over $Omega(G)$.

In this subsection, we develop Dress's theory of $G$-sets, following the
treatment in Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a and Dress~@bib:Dress1971a. We shall
occasionally omit details of proofs, especially in those cases where the module
analogue is already familiar to the reader.

Let $H <= G$, and let $S$ be a $G$-set. By restriction of operators from $G$ to
$H$, we obtain an $H$-set $op("res")_H^G S$, the _restriction of $S$_, also
denoted by $S_H$ for brevity. On the other hand, for each $H$-set $T$, we define
an _induced $G$-set_ $op("ind")_H^G T$ (or $T^G$ for brevity), as follows. We
make the cartesian product $G times T$ into a left $H$-set by setting
$ h(g, t) = (g h^(-1), h t) quad "for" h in H, g in G, t in T. $
The $H$-orbit $H(g, t)$ thus consists of all pairs ${(g h^(-1), h t): h in H}$.
Denote by $G times_H T$ the set of $H$-orbits in $G times T$, and make
$G times_H T$ into a left $G$-set by defining
$
  g' dot ("orbit of" (g, t)) = "orbit of" (g' g, t)
  quad "for" g, g' in G, t in T.
$
Now define $op("ind")_H^G T = G times_H T$, the $G$-set induced from $T$.
#idx("Induced G-set")

From the viewpoint of permutation modules, the above construction is quite
natural. Given an $H$-set $T$ and a commutative ring $R$, we form the
$R H$-permutation module $R[T]$. The induced $R G$-module is given by
$R G times.o_(R H) R[T]$, and clearly we have
$
  R G times.o_(R H) R[T] tilde.equiv R[op("ind")_H^G T]
  quad "for each" H"-set" T.
$
The basic properties of the restriction and induction maps on sets are as
follows:

#proposition[
  Let $H <= G$. Then
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        The restriction and induction maps are additive on disjoint unions of
        sets.
      ] <case:g-set-induction-restriction-additivity>],
    [
      #case[
        Let $H <= E <= G$, and let $S$ be an $H$-set. Then
        $ G times_E (E times_H S) tilde.equiv G times_H S quad "as" G"-sets", $
        that is, induction is transitive. Likewise, restriction is transitive.
      ] <case:g-set-induction-restriction-transitivity>],
    [
      #case[
        If $e_H$ is the $1$-point $H$-set, then
        $ G times_H e_H tilde.equiv G slash H quad "as left" G"-sets". $
      ] <case:induced-point-coset-g-set>],
  )
] <prop:g-set-induction-restriction-properties>
The proof is left as exercise for the reader. It follows from the above that
#source(864)
for $H <= G$, there are additive homomorphisms
$
  op("res")_H^G: Omega(G) -> Omega(H), quad op("ind")_H^G: Omega(H) -> Omega(G),
$
defined via restriction of $G$-sets and induction of $H$-sets, respectively.
Note that $op("res")_H^G$ is a ring homomorphism, but $op("ind")_H^G$ is not
(unless $H = G$).

We now consider the analogues of the Frobenius Reciprocity Theorems. Let
$H <= G$, and let $L$ and $M$ be $R G$- and $R H$-modules, respectively, where
$R$ is a commutative ring. In @i:th:frobenius-reciprocity and
@i:prop:frobenius-right-adjoint we showed that
$
  Hom_(R G) lr((M^G, L)) tilde.equiv Hom_(R H) lr((M, L_H)), quad
  Hom_(R G) lr((L, M^G)) tilde.equiv Hom_(R H) lr((L_H, M)).
$ <eq:module-frobenius-two-adjunctions>
The analogue of the first isomorphism is straightforward:

#proposition[
  Let $H <= G$, and let $S$ be an $H$-set, and $T$ a $G$-set. Then there is a
  set bijection
  $ Hom_G lr((S^G, T)) tilde.equiv Hom_H lr((S, T_H)). $
] <prop:g-set-induction-left-adjunction>
#proof[
  It suffices to treat the case where $S$ is a simple $H$-set, so let $S =
  H slash E$, where $E <= H$. Then
  $
    S^G = G times_H (H slash E) = G times_H (H times_E e_E) tilde.equiv G
    slash E.
  $
  Therefore $Hom_G lr((S^G, T)) tilde.equiv Hom_G lr((G slash E, T))
  tilde.equiv op("inv")_E T$ by @prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points. But also
  $Hom_H lr((H slash E, T_H)) tilde.equiv op("inv")_E T_H$ by
  @prop:g-set-maps-fixed-points, and the result follows.
]

The $G$-set version of the second isomorphism in
@eq:module-frobenius-two-adjunctions is more complicated, and will involve the
concept of tensor induction defined in §~@i:ss:tensor-induction. We postpone
this discussion to §~@ss:tensor-induction-algebraic-maps.

We shall now consider the $G$-set analogues of the Mackey theorems, using the
ordinary restriction and induction maps defined as in
@prop:g-set-induction-restriction-properties. Let $H <= G$, let $S$ be an
$H$-set, and $T$ a $G$-set; for convenience of notation, write
$ S^G = op("ind")_H^G S, quad T_H = op("res")_H^G T, $
when there is no danger of confusion. Then we have:

#theorem(name: "Subgroup Theorem")[
  Let $H, K <= G$, and let $S$ be an $H$-set. Then
  $
    (S^G)_K tilde.equiv union.dot.big_(G = union K a H)
    (attach(S, tl: a)_(attach(H, tl: a) inter K))^K
    quad "as left" K"-sets".
  $
  The union is taken over all $(K, H)$-double cosets $K a H$ of $G$, and for
  $a in G$, $attach(S, tl: a)$ denotes the $attach(H, tl: a)$-set defined by
  conjugation.
] <th:g-set-mackey-subgroup>
#source(865)
#proof[
  This follows from the proof of Mackey's Subgroup Theorem
  @i:th:mackey-subgroup, by calculating the $K$-orbits and stabilizers of
  elements in $S^G$. The reader may verify the details as an exercise. For the
  special case where $S = e_H$, see Exercise @exc:restricted-coset-g-set-orbits.
]

In a similar manner, we obtain the $G$-set analogue of Mackey's Tensor Product
Theorem @i:th:mackey-tensor-product, and again we omit the proof:

#theorem(name: "Tensor Product Theorem")[
  Let $H_i <= G$ and let $S_i$ be an $H_i$-set, $i = 1, 2$. Then
  $
    S_1^G times S_2^G tilde.equiv union.dot.big_(x^(-1) y in D)
    (
      (attach(S_1, tl: x) times attach(S_2, tl: y))_(attach(
        H_1,
        tl: x,
      ) inter attach(H_2, tl: y))
    )^G,
  $
  where $D$ ranges over all $(H_1, H_2)$-double cosets of $G$. There is one
  summand for each $D$, namely, we may choose any pair of elements $x, y in G$
  such that $x^(-1) y in D$.
] <th:g-set-mackey-tensor-product>

Note that Exercise @exc:coset-product-double-cosets is a special case of the
above result. Another special case, which is needed to show that $Omega(G)$ is a
Frobenius functor, is the analogue of @i:cor:induction-tensor-identity, namely:

#theorem(name: "Frobenius Reciprocity Theorem")[
  Let $H <= G$, and let $S$ be an $H$-set, $T$ a $G$-set. Then
  $ S^G times T tilde.equiv (S times T_H)^G quad "as" G"-sets". $
] <th:g-set-frobenius-reciprocity>

Starting with the concept of Frobenius functors, Green~@bib:Green1971 introduced
a somewhat richer axiomatic theory, which includes the idea of conjugation
within a group $G$, and the analogue of Mackey's Subgroup Theorem
@i:th:mackey-subgroup. Further, instead of considering all finite groups $G$
simultaneously, we focus our attention on the set of subgroups of a given group
$G$.

We begin with a preliminary definition. Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and $F$
an $R$-module. A _pairing_ is an $R$-bilinear map $F times F -> F$; the image of
$(x, y) in F times F$ is called the product $x y$. In general, we do not assume
that this multiplication is commutative, or even associative, and there need not
be an identity element. We call $F$ a _multiplicative $R$-module_. (For example,
every additive group is a multiplicative $ZZ$-module, with all products zero. As
another example, one can take the cohomology ring of a group, with
multiplication given by cup products.)
#idx("Multiplicative module")

#definition[
  Let $G$ be a finite group, and $R$ a commutative ring. A _$G$-functor_ (over
  $R$) consists of the data:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        A correspondence $F$ that assigns to each $H <= G$ a multiplicative
        $R$-module $F(H)$.
      ] <case:g-functor-multiplicative-modules>],
    [
      #source(866)
      #case[
        For $E <= H <= G$, there are maps
        $ op("res")_E^H: F(H) -> F(E), quad op("ind")_E^H: F(E) -> F(H), $
        which are transitive, and such that $op("res")_H^H = op("ind")_H^H = 1$.
      ] <case:g-functor-induction-restriction>],
    [
      #case[
        For each $H <= G$ and each $g in G$, let $H^g = g^(-1) H g$. There is an
        isomorphism (called _conjugation_)
        $ C_g^H: F(H) tilde.equiv F(H^g), $
        such that for $g, g' in G$ and $h in H$,
        $ C_(g')^(H^g) dot C_g^H = C_(g g')^H, quad "and" C_h^H = 1. $
      ] <case:g-functor-conjugation>],
    [
      #case[
        The restriction and induction maps satisfy the Frobenius identities (see
        @def:frobenius-functor part~@case:frobenius-functor-frobenius-identity)
        and commute with conjugation.
      ] <case:g-functor-frobenius-identities>],
    [
      #case[
        (Mackey condition.) Let $K <= G$, and let $E, H <= K$. Set
        $
          K = union.dot.big_(i=1)^n E g_i H, quad "and"
          E_i = E inter g_i H g_i^(-1), quad 1 <= i <= n.
        $
        Then for each $y in F(H)$,
        $
          (y^K)_E = sum_(i=1)^n (attach(y, tl: g_i)_(E_i))^E,
          quad "where" attach(y, tl: g) "means" C_(g^(-1))^H lr((y)).
        $
      ] <case:g-functor-mackey-condition>],
  )
] <def:g-functor>
#idx("G-functor", target: [@def:g-functor])

For example, let $H$ range over all subgroups of some fixed group $G$. Then
$Omega(H)$ and $op("ch")_QQ H$ are $G$-functors over $ZZ$, as is
$K_0 lr((ZZ H))$. Also, $QQ times.o_ZZ Omega(H)$ is a $G$-functor over $QQ$.

#definition[
  A $G$-functor $F$ is _multiplicative_ if each restriction map preserves
  products. In addition, we say that $F$ _has a unit_ if there exists a
  collection of elements ${u_H: H <= G}$, such that
  $
    u_H y = y u_H = y quad "for all" y in F(H), quad
    op("res")_E^H u_H = u_E quad "for" E <= H, \
    C_g^H u_H = u_(H^g) quad "for" g in G.
  $
] <def:multiplicative-unital-g-functor>

Thus, $Omega(H)$ and $op("ch")_QQ H$ are multiplicative $G$-functors with unit.
The functor $K_0 lr((ZZ H))$ is multiplicative but has no compatible unit family
when $G != 1$. For $G=1$ it is $K_0 lr((ZZ))=ZZ$, with its usual unit.

Given a multiplicative $G$-functor $F$ with unit, it is clear how to define the
concept of $F$-module, analogous to that of a Frobenius module over a Frobenius
functor. The results in §~@ss:grothendieck-frobenius carry over readily to
modules over $G$-functors, and we leave it to the reader to work out the
necessary details.
#idx("Frobenius module")
#idx("Induction map", target: [@case:g-functor-induction-restriction])

#source(867)
We note that for each commutative ring $R$, the Grothendieck group
$G_0 lr((R H))$ is a module over the functor $Omega(H)$. The action of
$Omega(H)$ on $G_0 lr((R H))$ is given as follows: for each $H$-set $S$, let
$R[S]$ be the permutation $R H$-module, with $R$-basis the elements of $S$. We
then define
$ [S] dot (M) = (R[S] times.o_R M) quad "for each" R H"-module" M. $
The Mackey theorems on modules then appear as formal consequences of the Mackey
theorems on sets. In this sense, the properties of Burnside rings relative to
induction, restriction, conjugation, and Mackey formulas provide a “universal”
combinatorial foundation for the corresponding concepts for various algebraic
objects associated with a group, such as character rings, Grothendieck groups,
projective class groups, cohomology rings, and so on. In particular, induction
theorems for Burnside rings then yield induction theorems for these various
other algebraic objects. We shall exploit this fact in §~@ss:conlon-induction
and §~@sec:representation-rings.

To illustrate this idea, we start with any set $cal(C)$ of subgroups of $G$, and
define
$
  Omega(G)_(cal(C)) = sum_(H in cal(C)) {Omega(H)}^G, quad
  Omega(G)^(cal(C)) = {x in Omega(G): x_H = 0 "for all" H in cal(C)},
$
so $Omega(G)_(cal(C))$ and $Omega(G)^(cal(C))$ are ideals of $Omega(G)$. We now
prove:

#theorem[
  Let $G$ be a group of order $n$, and $cal(C)$ any set of subgroups of $G$.
  Then
  $ n Omega(G) subset.eq Omega(G)^(cal(C)) plus.o Omega(G)_(cal(C)). $
] <th:burnside-induction-restriction-decomposition>
#proof[
  We shall use the results and notation of §~@ss:burnside-rings. Without loss of
  generality, we may enlarge $cal(C)$ so that if $E <=_G H in cal(C)$, then also
  $E in cal(C)$. (Call $cal(C)$ _subconjugately closed_ in this case.) By
  @cor:burnside-mark-cokernel-exponent we can choose $x, y in Omega(G)$ such
  that for $H <= G$,
  $
    phi_H lr((x)) = cases(n\, & H in cal(C)\,, 0\, & H in.not cal(C)\,)
    quad "and" quad
    phi_H lr((y)) = cases(0\, & H in cal(C)\,, n\, & H in.not cal(C).)
  $
  Then $n e_G = x + y$, and we shall prove that
  $x in Omega(G)_(cal(C)), y in Omega(G)^(cal(C))$. For the latter, we must show
  that $y_H = 0$ for each $H in cal(C)$. But for every $E <= H$ we have
  $E in cal(C)$, and therefore $phi_E lr((y_H)) = phi_E lr((y)) = 0$. Thus
  $y_H = 0$, by @prop:burnside-mark-full-lattice.

  Next, let $cal(S)$ be a full set of nonconjugate subgroups of $G$, and write
  $ x = sum_(H in cal(S)) n_(H)[G slash H], quad "where" n_H in ZZ. $
  To prove that $x in Omega(G)_(cal(C))$, it suffices to show that $n_H = 0$ for
  each $H in cal(S) - cal(C)$. If this is not the case, let
  $H in cal(S) - cal(C)$ be a maximal element (with respect to $<=_G$) among all
  such $H$ for which $n_H != 0$. Now let $K in cal(S)$, and suppose that
  $phi_H lr((n_(K)[G slash K])) != 0$. By @eq:burnside-mark-subconjugacy we have
  $H <=_G K$ and also $n_K != 0$, so $K = H$ by the maximality of $H$. It
  follows that $phi_H lr((n_(K)[G slash K])) = 0$ for $K in cal(S), K != H$. But
  then
  #source(868)
  $phi_H lr((x)) = n_H phi_(H)[G slash H] != 0$, contradicting the choice of
  $x$. This proves that $x in Omega(G)_(cal(C))$, so we deduce that
  $ n Omega(G) subset.eq Omega(G)^(cal(C)) + Omega(G)_(cal(C)). $
  The right-hand side is a direct sum, since if
  $z in Omega(G)^(cal(C)) inter Omega(G)_(cal(C))$, then $z^2 = 0$ by
  @prop:frobenius-family-ideals part~@case:frobenius-family-annihilation, and so
  $z = 0$ by @prop:burnside-mark-full-lattice.
]

#corollary[
  Let $M(G)$ be a Frobenius module over $Omega(G)$; then
  $ n dot M(G) subset.eq M(G)^(cal(C)) + M(G)_(cal(C)), $
  and $n dot (M(G)^(cal(C)) inter M(G)_(cal(C))) = 0$. In particular, if $n$
  acts invertibly on $M(G)$, then
  $ M(G) = M(G)^(cal(C)) plus.o M(G)_(cal(C)). $
] <cor:burnside-frobenius-module-decomposition>

=== Tensor Induction and Algebraic Maps
<ss:tensor-induction-algebraic-maps>

We shall define tensor induction of $H$-sets, where $H <= G$, and shall show how
this operation defines a multiplicative map from the Burnside ring $Omega(H)$ to
$Omega(G)$. The techniques are due to Dress, and we begin by recalling some
results from §~@i:ss:tensor-induction.

Let $H$ be a subgroup of $G$ of index $n$, and let
$ G = union.dot.big_(i=1)^n g_i H. $
For each $x in G$, we may write
$ x g_i = g_(pi(i)) h_i, quad "where" h_i in H, quad 1 <= i <= n, $
<eq:tensor-induced-coset-permutation>
and $pi in S_n$ is a permutation on ${1, dots, n}$. Let $H^n times.r S_n$ denote
the wreath product $H ≀ S_n$ (see §~@i:ss:tensor-induction). Then there is a
monomorphism
$
  phi: G -> H^n times.r S_n quad "given by"
  phi(x) = pi dot (h_1, dots, h_n),
$ <eq:tensor-induction-wreath-embedding>
using the notation in @eq:tensor-induced-coset-permutation.

Now let $T$ be any $H$-set, and let $T^n = T times dots times T$ ($n$ factors).
Then $H^n times.r S_n$ acts from the left as a permutation group on $T^n$, by
means of the formulas
$
  (h_1, dots, h_n)(t_1, dots, t_n)
  &= (h_1 t_1, dots, h_n t_n), quad h_i in H, t_i in T, \
  pi(t_1, dots, t_n)
  &= (t_(pi^(-1) lr((1))), dots, t_(pi^(-1) lr((n)))), quad pi in S_n.
$
Note that if $pi(j) = i$, then the $i$-th entry of $pi(t_1, dots, t_n)$ is
$t_j$. By virtue of the map $phi$ in @eq:tensor-induction-wreath-embedding, we
may view $T^n$ as a left $G$-set, called the _tensor-induced $G$-set_ obtained
from $T$, and denoted by $T^(times.o G)$. Specifically, for $x in G$ and
$(t_1, dots, t_n) in T^(times.o G)$,
#idx("Tensor induction")
#nidx("$T^(times.o G)$", "tensor induction")
#source(869)
we have
$
  x(t_1, dots, t_n) = pi(h_1, dots, h_n)(t_1, dots, t_n)
  = pi(h_1 t_1, dots, h_n t_n),
$
keeping the notation in @eq:tensor-induced-coset-permutation. In particular, we
have for $1 <= i <= n$ and $x in G$,
$
  i"-th entry of" x(t_1, dots, t_n) = h t_j,
  quad "where" x g_j = g_i h, h in H.
$ <eq:tensor-induced-entry-formula>
We shall see below that tensor induction on $H$-sets is closely related to
tensor induction of modules defined in §~@i:ss:tensor-induction.

We are now ready to prove the analogue of the second isomorphism in
@eq:module-frobenius-two-adjunctions.

#theorem(title: [Dress])[
  Let $H <= G$, let $S$ be a $G$-set, and $T$ an $H$-set. Then there is a
  bijection of sets
  $ Hom_H lr((S_H, T)) tilde.equiv Hom_G lr((S, T^(times.o G))). $
] <th:g-set-tensor-induction-right-adjunction>
#idx("Dress' theorem", target: [@th:g-set-tensor-induction-right-adjunction])
#proof[
  Given $F in Hom_H lr((S_H, T))$, we shall construct a corresponding map
  $F' in Hom_G lr((S, T^(times.o G)))$ as follows. Let $G = union.dot g_i H$ as
  above, and define
  $
    F': S -> T^(times.o G) quad "by"
    F'(s) = (F(g_1^(-1) s), dots, F(g_n^(-1) s)) quad "for" s in S.
  $
  Let us verify that $F'$ is a $G$-map, that is, $F'(x s) = x F'(s)$ for
  $x in G, s in S$. Keeping $x, s$ fixed, let $1 <= i <= n$. By
  @eq:tensor-induced-coset-permutation,
  $
    i"-th entry of" x F'(s) = h F(g_j^(-1) s)
    quad "where" x g_j = g_i h, h in H.
  $
  On the other hand,
  $
    i"-th entry of" F'(x s) = F(g_i^(-1) x s)
    = F(h g_j^(-1) s) = h F(g_j^(-1) s).
  $
  It follows that $F'(x s) = x F'(s)$, so $F'$ is a $G$-map, as claimed.

  Each $F$ determines a unique $F'$, and $F$ can be recovered from $F'$ in an
  obvious way. It remains for us to show that each
  $Phi in Hom_G lr((S, T^(times.o G)))$ is an $F'$ for some
  $F in Hom_H lr((S_H, T))$. Given $Phi$, we may write
  $ Phi(s) = (F_1 lr((g_1^(-1) s)), dots, F_n lr((g_n^(-1) s))), quad s in S, $
  with each $F_i$ a set map from $S$ into $T$. From the fact that $Phi$ is a
  $G$-map, it follows easily that
  $ F_1 = dots = F_n in Hom_H lr((S_H, T)), $
  and thus $Phi = (F_1)'$. This completes the proof.
]

We leave it to the reader to verify the following properties:

#source(870)
#proposition[
  Let $H <= G$, and let $T$ and $T'$ be $H$-sets.
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        There is an isomorphism of $G$-sets
        $
          (T times T')^(times.o G) tilde.equiv T^(times.o G) times
          (T')^(times.o G).
        $
      ] <case:tensor-induction-g-set-product>],
    [
      #case[
        Let $R$ be any commutative ring. There is an $R G$-isomorphism
        $ R[T^(times.o G)] tilde.equiv (R[T])^(times.o G) $
        of permutation modules, where the right-hand expression is a
        tensor-induced module defined as in @i:def:tensor-induction.
      ] <case:tensor-induction-permutation-module>],
  )
] <prop:tensor-induced-g-set-properties>

Now define
$
  Omega^+(G) = {sum a_(i)[S_i]: a_i in ZZ, a_i >= 0, S_i = G"-set"},
$ <eq:positive-burnside-semiring>
an additive semigroup of the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$. Note that $Omega^+(G)$ is
closed under multiplication. Each additive homomorphism $psi: Omega^+(G) -> A$,
where $A$ is an abelian group, extends uniquely to a homomorphism
$Omega(G) -> A$. Thus for $H <= G$, the maps $op("res")_H^G$ and
$op("ind")_H^G$, defined originally on $G$-sets (and hence on $Omega^+$), extend
to maps of Burnside rings. It will be important, in our later considerations, to
extend the tensor-induction map $Omega^+(H) -> Omega^+(G)$ to a map
$Omega(H) -> Omega(G)$. Since tensor induction is not additive, we must now
explain how such an extension can be defined. This will be done via Dress's
theory of “algebraic maps,” which we now describe.

_Algebraic Maps_

In this discussion, let $A$ be an additive semigroup with zero element, and let
$E$ be an additive group. We consider set maps $f: A -> E$, not necessarily
homomorphisms. Given such an $f$, for each $a in A$ we define
$ D_a f: A -> E quad "by" (D_a f)(x) = f(x + a) - f(x), quad x in A. $
Then $D_a D_b = D_b D_a$ for $a, b in A$. Further,
$
  (D_a D_b f)(x) = f(x+a+b) - f(x+a) - f(x+b) + f(x), quad x in A.
$ <eq:algebraic-map-second-difference>
We say that $f: A -> E$ is _algebraic of degree $n$_ if $n$ is the least integer
such that
$
  D_(a_1) D_(a_2) dots D_(a_(n+1)) f = 0 quad "for all" a_1, dots, a_(n+1) in A.
$
(Of course, for many maps $f$, no such $n$ exists.)
#idx("Algebraic map")

#remarks(numbered: true)[
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        $f$ is algebraic of degree $0$ if and only if $f$ is constant on $A$.
      ] <case:algebraic-degree-zero-constant>],
    [
      #case[
        A nonconstant map $f: A -> E$ is algebraic of degree $1$ if and only if
        #source(871)
        $f = h + c$, where $c$ is constant and $h: A -> E$ is a nonzero additive
        map. To prove this, we note that by @eq:algebraic-map-second-difference,
        $f$ is algebraic of degree $<= 1$ if and only if #named-equation(
          name: [$(ast)$],
        )[
          $
            f(x+a+b) = f(x+a) + f(x+b) - f(x)
            quad "for all" x, a, b in A.
          $
        ] <eq:degree-one-affine-difference>
        Clearly $f = h+c$ satisfies this condition. Conversely, if
        @eq:degree-one-affine-difference holds, then setting $x = 0$ in
        @eq:degree-one-affine-difference, and defining
        $h(y) = f(y)-f(0), y in A$, we obtain
        $ h(a+b) = h(a)+h(b) quad "for" a, b in A. $
        Thus $h$ is additive, and $f = h+f(0)$, as desired.
      ] <case:algebraic-degree-one-affine>],
    [
      #case[
        Let $f: RR -> RR$ be a polynomial map, given by $x in RR |-> f(x)$,
        where $f$ is a polynomial of degree $n$. If $n=0$, then $f$ is constant.
        For $n >= 1$ and $a != 0$, $D_a f$ has degree $n-1$, whereas $D_0 f=0$.
        All $(n+1)$-fold differences vanish, and the $n$-fold difference with
        unit steps equals $n!$ times the nonzero leading coefficient. Hence $f$
        is algebraic of degree $n$.
      ] <case:polynomial-algebraic-degree>],
  )
] <rem:algebraic-map-low-degrees>

The next example, involving $G$-sets, is of basic importance for our later
discussion. Let $H <= G$, and put $n = abs(G : H)$. Then $Omega^+(H)$ is an
additive semigroup (see @eq:positive-burnside-semiring), and $Omega(G)$ is an
additive group. There is then a map (tensor-induction)
$
  times.o G: Omega^+(H) -> Omega(G), quad "given by" [S] |-> [S^(times.o G)]
$ <eq:positive-burnside-tensor-induction>
#idx("Tensor induction", target: [@eq:positive-burnside-tensor-induction])
for every $H$-set $S$. We now prove:

#proposition(title: [Dress])[
  Let $H <= G$, $n = abs(G : H)$. Then the tensor-induction map, defined above,
  is an algebraic map of degree $n$.
] <prop:tensor-induction-algebraic-degree>
#proof[
  Let $S, T$ be $H$-sets; each element of $S^(times.o G)$ is an ordered
  $n$-tuple of elements of $S$. Let $f: Omega^+(H) -> Omega(G)$ be the map given
  by $f[S] = [S^(times.o G)]$. Then
  $
    (D_T f)[S] = f[S union.dot T] - f[S]
    = [(S union.dot T)^(times.o G)] - [S^(times.o G)].
  $
  But we may write
  $
    (S union.dot T)^(times.o G) = S^(times.o G) union.dot K_1,
    quad "where" K_1 = K_1 lr((S,T)),
  $
  and $K_1 lr((S,T))$ is the $G$-subset of $(S union.dot T)^n$ of ordered
  $n$-tuples having at least one entry in $T$. Clearly
  $(D_T f)[S] = [K_1 lr((S,T))]$ in $Omega(G)$.

  Now let $S, T, T'$ be $H$-sets. Then
  $
    (D_(T') D_T f)[S] = [K_1 lr((S union.dot T',T))] - [K_1 lr((S,T))]
    = [K_2 lr((S,T,T'))]
  $
  in $Omega(G)$, where $K_2 lr((S,T,T'))$ is the $G$-subset of
  $(S union.dot T union.dot T')^n$ consisting of all $n$-tuples from
  $S union.dot T union.dot T'$ having at least one entry from $T$ and one from
  $T'$. Continuing in this fashion, the $n$-fold difference is independent of
  $S$, and every $(n+1)$-fold difference vanishes. Choose each $T_i$ to be the
  one-point $H$-set. The resulting $n$-fold difference is the class of a
  nonempty $G$-set with $n!$ elements, and is therefore nonzero in $Omega(G)$.
  #source(872)
  Thus $f$ is algebraic of degree exactly $n$, as claimed.
]

Given an additive semigroup $A$ with zero element, let $overline(A)$ be the
additive group generated by the elements of $A$. To define $overline(A)$, we
introduce an equivalence relation on $A times A$ by writing
$
  (x,y) tilde (x',y') quad "if and only if"
  x+y'+z = x'+y+z quad "for some" z in A.
$
Let $overline(A)$ be the set of equivalence classes of ordered pairs, and denote
the class of $(x,y)$ by $x-y$. Then $overline(A)$ is an additive group, and
there is an additive map $A -> overline(A)$, given by
$a in A |-> (a+z,z) in overline(A)$ for any $z in A$. For $a in A$, we denote
the class of $(a+z,z)$ by $overline(a) in overline(A)$, and there is then an
additive homomorphism $A -> overline(A)$ given by $a |-> overline(a)$, which
need not be injective, generally speaking.

Now let $f: A -> E$ be a map, not necessarily additive, from the additive
semigroup $A$ into an additive group $E$. We wish to find a map
$overline(f): overline(A) -> E$ extending $f$; note that $overline(f)$ need not
be a homomorphism. We give two examples:

#case-list(
  numbering: "(i)",
  [
    #case[
      Given a constant map $f: A -> E$, with $f(a) = c in E$ for all $a in A$,
      define $overline(f)(x-y) = c$ for all $x,y in A$. Then $overline(f)$ is a
      constant map that extends $f$.
    ] <case:constant-map-group-completion-extension>],
  [
    #case[
      Let $f = h+c$, where $h$ is additive and $c$ is constant. We set
      $ overline(f)(x-y) = h(x)-h(y)+c, quad "for" x-y in overline(A). $
      It is easily checked that $overline(f)$ is well-defined, and extends $f$.
    ] <case:affine-map-group-completion-extension>],
)
More generally, we prove:

#theorem(title: [Dress])[
  Let $f: A -> E$ be an algebraic map of degree $n$ from an additive semigroup
  $A$ into an additive group $E$. Let $overline(A)$ be the additive group
  generated by $A$. Then there is a unique algebraic extension
  $overline(f): overline(A) -> E$ of $f$, and its degree is $n$.
] <th:algebraic-map-group-completion-extension>
#idx("Dress' theorem", target: [@th:algebraic-map-group-completion-extension])
#proof[
  We shall give a “symbolic” proof that captures the essence of the
  construction; a detailed proof, via induction on $n$, is given in
  Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a, pp.~60–76. Suppose that $overline(f)$ has been
  constructed in some way, and let $x,y in A$. Then
  $
    {(1+D_y) overline(f)}(x-y) & = overline(f)(x-y)+(D_y overline(f))(x-y) \
                               & = overline(f)(x-y)+f(x)-overline(f)(x-y)
                                 = f(x),
  $
  that is,
  $ overline(f)(x-y) = (1+D_y)^(-1) f(x) = (1-D_y+D_y^2-dots) f(x). $
  Since $D_y^(n+1) = 0$, we obtain for $x,y in A$,
  #source(873)
  $
    overline(f)(x-y) & = f(x)-(D_y f)(x)+(D_y^2 f)(x)-dots \
                     & = f(x)-{f(x+y)-f(x)} \
                     & quad +{f(x+2 y)-2 f(x+y)+f(x)}+dots;
  $ <eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension>
  a terminating series. Note that when $y = 0$, this gives
  $overline(f)(x) = f(x)$.

  We shall define $overline(f)$ by
  @eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension, and must verify that if
  $(x,y) tilde (x',y')$ in $A times A$, then
  $overline(f)(x-y) = overline(f)(x'-y')$. Increasing both $x$ and $y$ by some
  $z in A$, and changing notation, we may assume that $x+y' = x'+y$. We must
  prove that
  $ {(1+D_y)^(-1) f}(x) = {(1+D_(y'))^(-1) f}(x') quad "in" E. $
  Since $D_y$ and $D_(y')$ commute, it suffices to check that
  $ {(1+D_(y')) f}(x) = {(1+D_y) f}(x'), $
  that is, $f(x+y') = f(x'+y)$. But this is clear, so we have now shown that
  $overline(f): overline(A) -> E$ is a well-defined extension of $f$.

  Finally, let us prove that $overline(f)$ is algebraic of degree $n$. For
  $x,y,z in A$, we have
  $ (D_(y-z) overline(f))(x) = (D_y overline(f))(x-z)-(D_z overline(f))(x-z). $
  From this and @eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension it follows at once
  that $overline(f)$ is algebraic of degree $<= n$. The degree cannot be less
  than $n$, since $overline(f)$ extends $f$. The finite inverse-difference
  formula forces the values of any algebraic extension, so the extension is
  unique. This completes the proof.
]

#remarks(numbered: false)[
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        If $f$ is constant, then so is $overline(f)$ by
        @eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension, and
        $overline(f)(x-y) = f(x)$.
      ] <case:constant-algebraic-extension>],
    [
      #case[
        For $xi, eta in overline(A)$, the analogue of
        @eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension is valid, that is,
        $ overline(f)(xi-eta) = {(1-D_eta+D_eta^2-dots) overline(f)}(xi). $
        <eq:algebraic-extension-general-difference>
      ] <case:algebraic-extension-general-difference>],
  )
] <rem:algebraic-extension-difference-formulas>

Suppose now that for each $x,y in A$, there is a product $x y in A$, and that
this multiplication is commutative, associative, and satisfies the distributive
law. Then there is a well-defined multiplication in $overline(A)$, and
$overline(A)$ becomes a commutative ring (not necessarily having an identity
element).

#theorem[
  Let $A$ be an additive semigroup with products, and let $f: A -> E$ be an
  algebraic map from $A$ into a commutative ring $E$. Suppose that $f$ is
  multiplicative, that is,
  $
    f(x y) = f(x) f(y)
    quad "for all" x,y in A.
  $
  Then the unique extension $overline(f)$ of $f$ to $overline(A)$ is also
  multiplicative.
] <th:algebraic-extension-preserves-multiplication>
#proof[
  For fixed $y in A$, there is an algebraic map $phi: A -> E$, defined by
  $ phi(x) = f(x) f(y), quad x in A. $
  #source(874)
  Then $phi$ lifts uniquely to a map $overline(phi): overline(A) -> E$. But
  clearly the formula
  $ overline(phi)(xi) = overline(f)(xi) f(y), quad xi in overline(A), $
  gives a lifting of $phi$. On the other hand, since $phi(x) = f(x y)$ for all
  $x in A$, $overline(phi)$ is also given by
  $ overline(phi)(xi) = overline(f)(xi y), quad xi in overline(A). $
  This implies that
  $
    overline(f)(xi y) = overline(f)(xi) f(y)
    quad "for all" xi in overline(A), y in A.
  $
  Now keep $xi in overline(A)$ fixed, and let $psi: A -> E$ be the algebraic map
  for which $psi(y) = overline(f)(xi) f(y), y in A$. Then $psi$ lifts to a map
  $overline(psi)$ on $overline(A)$, and as above we obtain
  $
    overline(f)(xi) overline(f)(eta) = overline(f)(xi eta)
    quad "for all" xi,eta in overline(A).
  $
  This completes the proof.
]

We now apply this result to the study of Burnside rings. To begin with, the
additive semigroup $Omega^+(G)$ defined in @eq:positive-burnside-semiring is
embedded in the Burnside ring $Omega(G)$, by virtue of
@lem:burnside-g-set-cancellation. From @prop:tensor-induction-algebraic-degree
and @th:algebraic-extension-preserves-multiplication we obtain at once:

#theorem(title: [Dress])[
  Let $H <= G$ be finite groups. There is a unique algebraic multiplicative map
  $phi': Omega(H) -> Omega(G)$, called _tensor induction_, such that
  $ phi'[S] = [S^(times.o G)] quad "for each" H"-set" S. $
  The map $phi'$ is algebraic of degree $abs(G : H)$.
] <th:burnside-tensor-induction-extension>
#idx("Dress' theorem", target: [@th:burnside-tensor-induction-extension])
#idx("Tensor induction", target: [@th:burnside-tensor-induction-extension])

We remark that $phi'$ is not additive if $H < G$. Next, for $y in Omega(H)$ we
denote $phi'(y)$ by $y^(times.o G)$. For any $H$-sets $S$ and $T$, it follows
from @eq:algebraic-map-finite-difference-extension that
$
  ([S]-[T])^(times.o G)
  &= [S^(times.o G)]-{[(S union.dot T)^(times.o G)]-[S^(times.o G)]} \
  &quad +{[(S union.dot T union.dot T)^(times.o G)]
    -2[(S union.dot T)^(times.o G)]+[S^(times.o G)]}-dots,
$
and only finitely many braces are nonzero. The following description by marks
applies to these virtual differences.

#corollary[
  Let $H$ be a subgroup of $G$ of index $n$, and let $S,T$ be $H$-sets. For each
  $i$, $0 <= i <= n$, let $V_i$ be the $G$-subset of
  $(S union.dot T)^(times.o G)$ consisting of the tuples having exactly $i$
  entries from $S$ and $n-i$ entries from $T$. For $L <= G$ and a representative
  $g$ of each double coset in $L backslash G slash H$, put
  $H_g=H inter g^(-1) L g$. Then
  $
    phi_(L) lr((([S]-[T])^(times.o G))) =
    product_(g in L backslash G slash H)
    lr((phi_(H_g)[S]-phi_(H_g)[T])).
  $
  The marks on the right are those of $H$.
  #ed-note[The originally printed alternating sum of the $[V_i]$ is false. For
    $G=C_2$, $H=1$, and $S=T$ a point, it gives $2[G slash G]-[G slash 1]$,
    whose $G$-mark is $2$, although the norm of $0$ is $0$. A subgroup can
    permute the tuple positions, so the ordinary parity of the number of
    positions assigned to $T$ does not supply the required signs.]
] <cor:tensor-induction-alternating-colored-subsets>
#source(875)
#proof[
  For an actual $H$-set $U$, @th:g-set-tensor-induction-right-adjunction
  identifies the $L$-fixed points of $U^(times.o G)$ with the $H$-maps from
  $(G slash L)_H$ to $U$. Its $H$-orbits have stabilizers $H_g$, using the
  representatives $g^(-1) L$. Consequently
  $
    phi_(L)[U^(times.o G)] =
    product_(g in L backslash G slash H) phi_(H_g)[U].
  $
  Both sides, as functions of $[U]$, are algebraic maps of degree at most $n$:
  the left side by @th:burnside-tensor-induction-extension, the right side as a
  product of at most $n$ additive marks. They agree on $Omega^+(H)$, and hence
  agree on $Omega(H)$ by @th:algebraic-map-group-completion-extension.
  Substitution of $[S]-[T]$ gives the assertion. The injectivity of the combined
  marks also shows that these formulas determine the integral norm uniquely.
]

=== Conlon's Induction Theorem <ss:conlon-induction>

Throughout let $K$ be a field of characteristic $p >= 0$, and let $G$ be a
finite group. We shall prove Conlon's generalization of the Artin Induction
Theorem, following the proof given by Dress~@bib:Dress1973, with minor
simplifications. The calculations will take place in the _representation ring_
$a(K G)$, also called the _Green ring_, defined as follows. Restricting our
attention to f.g. left $K G$-modules, let $a(K G)$ be the ring consisting of
$ZZ$-linear combinations of symbols $[M]$, one for each isomorphism class of
$K G$-modules $M$, with relations given by direct sum:
$[M plus.o M'] = [M]+[M']$ for $K G$-modules $M$ and $M'$. Multiplication is
defined by inner tensor products (over $K$) of $K G$-modules. Since the
Krull–Schmidt–Azumaya Theorem holds for $K G$-modules, it follows that $a(K G)$
has a $ZZ$-basis consisting of all $[M]$ with $M$ indecomposable. Further, for
$K G$-modules $N$ and $N'$, we have $[N] = [N']$ in $a(K G)$ if and only if
$N tilde.equiv N'$.
#idx("Representation ring")
#nidx("$a(G)$", "representation ring = Green ring")

It is convenient to introduce the _representation algebra_
$ A(K G) = QQ times.o_ZZ a(K G). $
#idx("Representation algebra")
#nidx("$A(G)$", "representation algebra")
Then $a(K G)$ is embedded in $A(K G)$ as a subring, and both have the same
identity element $[K_G]$, where $K_G$ is the field $K$ on which $G$ acts
trivially. There are induction maps $a(K H) -> a(K G)$, and $A(K H) -> A(K G)$,
for each $H <= G$. As usual, for $x in a(K H)$, let $x^G$ denote its image in
$a(K G)$. However, we shall avoid using a subscript $H$ to denote restriction,
in view of our notation $K_G$ just introduced.

Our object is to express the element $[K_G] in A(K G)$ as a $QQ$-linear
combination of induced elements $[K_H]^G$, with $H$ ranging over a suitably
chosen family of subgroups of $G$. If $char K = 0$, it suffices to let $H$ range
over all cyclic subgroups of $G$, by the Artin Induction Theorem
@i:th:artin-induction-explicit-coefficients. For $char K = p > 0$, we need
instead the family $cal(H)'$ of all _$p$-hypo-elementary_ subgroups of $G$,
given as follows:
#idx("Artin Induction Theorem")

#definition[
  A group $H$ is _$p$-hypo-elementary_, or _cyclic mod $p$_, if
  $H = P times.r C$, where $P$ is a normal $p$-subgroup and $C$ is a cyclic
  $p'$-group. (For $p = 0$, $H$ may be any cyclic group.) By way of contrast,
  the $p$-hyper-elementary groups have the form $C times.r P$, with $C, P$ as
  above.
] <def:p-hypo-elementary-group>
#idx("Hypo-elementary group", target: [@def:p-hypo-elementary-group])
#idx("Cyclic mod p", see: ("Hypo-elementary group",))

The main result is as follows:

#theorem(name: "Conlon Induction Theorem")[
  #footnote[See also @th:conlon-p-permutation-induction.]
  Let $K$ be a field of characteristic $p$, and let $cal(H)'$ be the set of
  $p$-hypo-elementary subgroups of a given group $G$. Then there is a relation
  in $A(K G)$:
  $
    [K_G] = sum_(H in cal(H)') alpha_(H)[K_H]^G
    quad "for some rational numbers" {alpha_H}.
  $
  <eq:conlon-trivial-representation-induction>
] <th:conlon-permutation-induction>
#idx("Conlon's Induction Theorem", target: [@th:conlon-permutation-induction])
#source(876)
#proof[
  #case-list(
    prefix: "Step ",
    numbering: "1.",
    [
      #case[
        We first consider the _Burnside algebra_ $Omega_QQ lr((K G))$, defined
        as the $QQ$-subalgebra of $A(K G)$ generated by all permutation
        representations of $G$ over $K$. Since each $G$-set is a disjoint union
        of left $G$-sets $G slash H$ with $H <= G$, and since the permutation
        module $K[G slash H]$ is $K G$-isomorphic to the induced module
        $(K_H)^G$, it follows that
        $ Omega_QQ lr((K G)) = sum_(H <= G) QQ dot [K_H]^G. $
        <eq:burnside-permutation-algebra-span>
        #idx("Burnside algebra")
        For convenience of notation, let us set $F(H) = Omega_QQ lr((K H))$ for
        each $H <= G$. Then in the terminology of §~@ss:g-sets-induction-maps,
        $F$ is a multiplicative $G$-functor with unit ${[K_H]: H <= G}$. Next,
        we note that
        $
          sum_(H in cal(H)') F(H)^G
          &= {sum_(H in cal(H)') (sum_(E <= H) QQ dot [K_E]^H)}^G \
          &= sum_(E <= H in cal(H)') QQ dot [K_E]^G.
        $
        If $E <= H in cal(H)'$, then $E in cal(H)'$ (see Exercise
        @exc:hypo-elementary-subgroups-quotients), and therefore
        $ sum_(H in cal(H)') F(H)^G = sum_(H in cal(H)') QQ dot [K_H]^G. $
        The left-hand side, usually denoted by $F(G)_(cal(H)')$, is an ideal of
        the ring $F(G)$. Thus, the existence of a formula
        @eq:conlon-trivial-representation-induction is equivalent to the
        assertion that
        $ F(G)_(cal(H)') = F(G). $
      ] <case:conlon-burnside-algebra-reduction>],
    [
      #case[
        Here we make some general observations about induction theorems of the
        above type. Let $F$ be any $G$-functor, which assigns to each $H <= G$ a
        commutative ring $F(H)$ with unit $u_H$, as in Definition
        @def:multiplicative-unital-g-functor. We wish to show that
        $F(G)_(cal(C)) = F(G)$ for some suitably chosen class $cal(C)$ of
        subgroups of $G$.

        We set
        $ F'(G) = sum_(H < G) op("ind")_H^G F(H) = "ideal of" F(G). $
        If $F'(G) = F(G)$, we call $G$ “good”; if $F'(G)$ is a proper ideal of
        $F(G)$, we call $G$ “bad.” This terminology is temporary and is helpful
        in stating the argument that follows. Obviously we have
        $
          F(G) = sum_(H <= G) op("ind")_H^G F(H) = sum_(H <= G, H "bad")
          op("ind")_H^G F(H).
        $
        Thus, $F(G)_(cal(C)) = F(G)$ whenever $cal(C)$ contains all bad
        $H <= G$.

        In practice, we choose a collection $cal(C)$ of subgroups of $G$, and
        try to prove that
        #source(877)
        each $H <= G$, for which $F'(H) != F(H)$, must lie in $cal(C)$. This is
        usually accomplished in three steps:
        #case-list(
          numbering: "(i)",
          [
            #case[Find a collection $cal(C)^ast$ of good groups.]
            <case:conlon-good-group-obstructions>],
          [
            #case[Show that homomorphic images of bad groups are bad.]
            <case:conlon-bad-group-quotient-closure>],
          [
            #case[Show that subgroups of bad groups are bad.]
            <case:conlon-bad-group-subgroup-closure>],
        )
        It will then follow that if $H$ is a bad subgroup of $G$, then no
        subgroup or homomorphic image of $H$ can lie in $cal(C)^ast$. A
        group-theoretic argument is then used to prove that $H in cal(C)$. Note
        that the trivial group ${1}$ is necessarily bad.

        The proofs of @case:conlon-bad-group-quotient-closure and
        @case:conlon-bad-group-subgroup-closure, for the cases needed here,
        depend on a pair of lemmas, which we now establish.

        #lemma[
          Let $overline(H) = H slash N$, where $N ⊴ H$, and set
          $overline(E) = E slash N$
          for each $E$ with $N <= E <= H$. Suppose that for each $H$, there is
          an inflation map $F(overline(H)) -> F(H)$, which is a ring
          homomorphism such that
          $
            u_(overline(H)) |-> u_H,
            quad "and" F(overline(E))^(overline(H)) |-> F(E)^H.
          $
          Then if $H$ is bad, so is $overline(H)$.
        ] <lem:conlon-bad-groups-quotient-closure>
        #proof[
          Assume $overline(H)$ good, so $F'(overline(H)) = F(overline(H))$, or
          equivalently,
          $
            u_(overline(H)) in
            sum_(overline(E) < overline(H)) F(overline(E))^(overline(H)).
          $
          Applying the inflation map to the above, we obtain $u_H in F'(H)$, so
          $H$ is good. This completes the proof.
        ]

        #lemma[
          Suppose that for $H <= G$, there is a multiplicative map
          $mu: F(H) -> F(G)$ such that
          $
            mu(u_H) = u_G, quad mu(F'(H)) subset.eq F'(G), \
            mu(x+y) equiv mu(x)+mu(y) (mod F'(G)) quad "for" x,y in F(H).
          $
          Then $mu$ induces a ring homomorphism
          $ F(H) slash F'(H) -> F(G) slash F'(G). $
          Therefore if $G$ is bad, so is $H$.
        ] <lem:conlon-bad-groups-norm-subgroup-closure>
        #proof[
          A group $G$ is good if and only if $u_G in F'(G)$, that is,
          $F(G) slash F'(G)$ is the zero ring. If $H$ is good, then $u_H in
          F'(H)$, so $u_G = mu(u_H) in F'(G)$, and therefore $G$ is good.
        ]
      ] <case:conlon-good-bad-g-functors>],
    [
      #case[
        Let us apply the above discussion to the case where
        $F(H) = Omega_QQ lr((K H))$, and begin by verifying the hypotheses of
        the lemmas. Let $overline(H) = H slash N$ as in
        @lem:conlon-bad-groups-quotient-closure. The inflation map carries
        $K_(overline(H))$ into $K_H$, and induces a ring homomorphism
        #source(878)
        $F(overline(H)) -> F(H)$. Further,
        $
          "inflation of" (K_(overline(E)))^(overline(H)) tilde.equiv (K_E)^H
          quad "as" K H"-modules".
        $
        It follows from @lem:conlon-bad-groups-quotient-closure that if $H$ is
        bad, so is $overline(H)$.

        Second, let $H <= G$ and consider the tensor induction map
        $Omega(H) -> Omega(G)$ defined in
        @th:burnside-tensor-induction-extension.
        #ed-note[The printed proof passes directly from the Burnside norm to the
          rational permutation representation image. A relation in the lattice
          Green group can involve a stable complement that is not a permutation
          module, so descent through that image needs a separate justification.]
        To justify descent to the representation algebra, first apply tensor
        induction to the monoid of all $R H$-lattices that are finite projective
        over $R$, with $R=K$ here. Tensor products preserve finite
        $R$-projectivity, and the decomposition by tuple colors shows that this
        map is algebraic of degree at most $abs(G : H)$. Thus
        @th:algebraic-map-group-completion-extension extends it to the full
        group $a(R H)$, respecting arbitrary stable lattice isomorphisms.

        Let $P(H)$ be the subgroup generated by permutation-module classes. The
        finite-difference formula keeps the norm of $P(H)$ inside $P(G)$. These
        groups are finitely generated. After tensoring the target with $QQ$,
        torsion translations act trivially: a finite-order unipotent operator is
        the identity in characteristic zero. On the free quotient, the finite
        mixed Newton expansion gives a rational polynomial, which extends
        uniquely from the integer lattice to its rational span. Multiplicativity
        and the unit identity extend as polynomial identities. We therefore
        obtain a well-defined multiplicative map $mu:F(H) -> F(G)$ satisfying
        $ mu[M] = [M^(times.o G)] quad "for" M=(K_E)^H, E <= H, $
        and $mu[K_H]=[K_G]$.

        If $H=G$, then $mu$ is the identity and both assertions below are
        immediate. Suppose $H<G$. For actual permutation modules the mixed color
        summands are induced from proper stabilizers, by the analogue of
        Exercise @exc:tensor-induction-mixed-subsets-induced. The difference
        $mu(x+y)-mu(x)-mu(y)$, followed by the quotient map to
        $F(G) slash F'(G)$, is a rational polynomial in the coordinates of
        $x,y$. It vanishes on all nonnegative integral sums of the finitely many
        permutation generators. Hence it vanishes identically, and
        $ mu(x+y) equiv mu(x)+mu(y) (mod F'(G)) quad "for" x,y in F(H). $

        The resulting map $F(H) -> F(G) slash F'(G)$ is additive and therefore
        $QQ$-linear. By the analogue of Exercise
        @exc:tensor-induction-proper-induced-set it kills each class induced
        from an actual permutation module over a proper subgroup of $H$. These
        classes span $F'(H)$ over $QQ$, so $mu(F'(H)) subset.eq F'(G)$. The
        hypotheses of @lem:conlon-bad-groups-norm-subgroup-closure are therefore
        satisfied in this case, and we may conclude that subgroups of bad groups
        are also bad.
      ] <case:conlon-inflation-tensor-norm>],
    [
      #case[
        It remains for us to construct a collection $cal(C)^ast$ of good groups
        (for the choice $F(H) = Omega_QQ lr((K H))$), and using this collection,
        to show that every bad group must lie in the set $cal(H)'$ of
        $p$-hypo-elementary subgroups of $G$. We assume from now on that
        $p > 0$, since @eq:conlon-trivial-representation-induction follows from
        the Artin Induction Theorem when $p = 0$.

        #lemma[
          Let $H = C times C$, where $C$ is cyclic of prime order $q$, and
          $q != p$. Then there exist integers ${m_E: E < H}$ such that
          $ q[K_H] = sum_(E < H) m_(E)[K_E]^H quad "in" a(K H). $
          <eq:elementary-abelian-good-group-relation>
          Therefore $H$ is good (relative to $Omega_QQ lr((K H))$).
        ] <lem:conlon-elementary-abelian-good-group>
        #proof[
          In fact the required relation holds over every commutative ring $R$ in
          which $q$ is invertible.
          #ed-note[The printed proof passes from a rational representation
            relation to an integral lattice relation by a citation. Such a
            passage needs justification: equality of rational characters alone
            does not imply equality in the split Green group of lattices.]
          Let $R_H$ denote the trivial $R H$-module. Let $cal(U)$ be the $q+1$
          subgroups of $H$ of order $q$. For $V=H$ or $V in cal(U)$, set
          $ e_V=abs(V)^(-1) sum_(v in V) v quad "in" R H. $
          If $U$ and $V$ are distinct members of $cal(U)$, then $e_U e_V=e_H$.
          Direct coefficient counting gives
          $ sum_(U in cal(U)) e_U=1+q e_H. $
          Hence the idempotents $e_U-e_H$ are pairwise orthogonal and sum to
          $1-e_H$. Since $R[H slash U] tilde.equiv R H e_U$, we have
          $
            R H = R H e_H plus.o
            product.co_(U in cal(U)) R H(e_U-e_H), \
            R[H slash U] tilde.equiv R_H plus.o R H(e_U-e_H).
          $
          It follows that
          $
            product.co_(U in cal(U)) R[H slash U]
            tilde.equiv R_H^q plus.o R H,
          $
          and therefore
          $
            q[R_H] = sum_(U in cal(U)) [R_U]^H - [R_1]^H
            quad "in" a(R H).
          $
          Taking $R=K$ proves @eq:elementary-abelian-good-group-relation and the
          lemma.
        ]

        #lemma[
          Let $H = C times.r A$, where $C$ is cyclic of prime order $q != p$,
          and $A = Aut C$. Let $H_0 = C times.r A_0$, where $1 < A_0 <= A$. Then
          there exist integers
          #source(879)
          ${m_E: E < H}$ such that
          $
            (q-1)[K_(H_0)] = sum_(E < H_0) m_(E)[K_E]^(H_0) quad "in" a(K H_0),
          $
          <eq:cyclic-prime-affine-good-group-relation>
          and thus $H_0$ is good (relative to $Omega_QQ lr((K H_0))$).
        ] <lem:conlon-cyclic-prime-affine-good-group>
        #proof[
          Let $C = chevron.l c: c^q = 1 chevron.r$,
          $A = chevron.l a: a^(q-1) = 1 chevron.r$, and $a c a^(-1) = c^m$,
          where $m$ is a primitive root $(mod q)$. Let
          $
            R = ZZ_((p)), quad S = R[omega],
            quad omega = "primitive" q"-th root of" 1.
          $
          If $S_A$ denotes $S$ on which $A$ acts trivially, we have
          $ S H times.o_(S A) S_A tilde.equiv S C, $
          where $A$ acts by conjugation on $S C$. But $q in S^times$, so as
          $S C$-modules,
          $
            S C tilde.equiv product.co_(i=0)^(q-1) S_i,
            quad "where" S_i = S "on which" c "acts as" omega^i.
          $
          Then $S_0$ is the $H$-trivial module $S_H$, while
          $a dot S_i = S_(m^(-1) i)$ for $1 <= i <= q-1$, where the inverse is
          taken modulo $q$. Thus $product.co_1^(q-1) S_i$ is a system of
          imprimitivity for $H$, and the stabilizer of $S_1$ is $C$. It follows
          that $product.co_1^(q-1) S_i tilde.equiv S H times.o_(S C) S_1$, and
          therefore
          $
            S H times.o_(S A) S_A tilde.equiv S_H plus.o (S H times.o_(S C) S_1)
            quad "as" S H"-modules".
          $
          Now $S$ is $R$-free of rank $q-1$. In the above, we restrict operators
          to $R H_0$, obtaining a relation in $a(R H_0)$: #named-equation(
            name: [$(ast)$],
          )[
            $
              (q-1) op("res")_(H_0)^H op("ind")_A^H [R_A]
              = (q-1)[R_(H_0)] + op("res")_(H_0)^H op("ind")_C^H [S_1].
            $
          ] <eq:conlon-affine-cyclotomic-lattice-relation>
          However, $R C tilde.equiv R_C plus.o S_1$, where $C$ acts trivially on
          $R_C$, so @eq:conlon-affine-cyclotomic-lattice-relation becomes
          $
            (q-1) op("res")_(H_0)^H op("ind")_A^H [R_A] + op("res")_(H_0)^H
            op("ind")_C^H [R_C]
            = (q-1)[R_(H_0)] + op("res")_(H_0)^H op("ind")_({1})^H [R_({1})].
          $
          The Mackey Subgroup Theorem then yields a relation of the form
          @eq:cyclic-prime-affine-good-group-relation, with $R$ in place of $K$
          throughout. Since $K$ is an extension field of $R slash p R$, the rest
          of the proof is as above.
        ]
      ] <case:conlon-good-group-obstructions-explicit>],
    [
      #case[
        We have now established that for each prime $q != p$, the groups
        $C times C$ and $C times.r A_0$ are good (for the functor
        $F(H) = Omega_QQ lr((K H))$), where $C$ is cyclic of order $q$, and
        $A_0$ is any nontrivial subgroup of $Aut C$. We must show that every bad
        group $H$ is $p$-hypo-elementary.

        Let $q$ be any prime dividing $abs(H)$, $q != p$. Since $H$ is bad by
        hypothesis, each Sylow $q$-subgroup $H_q$ of $H$ must also be bad.
        Therefore $H_q$ must be cyclic, since
        #source(880)
        otherwise $H_q$ would have a factor group of the form $C times C$, where
        $abs(C) = q$, which is impossible by Lemma
        @lem:conlon-elementary-abelian-good-group. We now show that $H$ contains
        a normal $q$-complement, that is, there exists an $N_q ⊴ H$ with
        $abs(N_q) = q'"-part of" abs(H)$. By the Burnside Transfer Theorem
        @i:th:burnside-normal-p-complement, to show the existence of $N_q$, we
        need only verify that $C_H lr((H_q)) = N_H lr((H_q))$. Omitting the
        subscript $H$ for brevity, suppose that there exists an
        $x in N(H_q)-C(H_q)$, and let us obtain a contradiction. Write
        $x = x' y$, where $x'$ is a $q$-element, $y$ a $q'$-element, with
        $x', y$ powers of $x$. Then $x' in N(H_q)$, and therefore $x' in H_q$
        since $H_q$ is a Sylow $q$-subgroup of $H$. Since $H_q$ is cyclic, $x'$
        centralizes $H_q$, and therefore $y in N(H_q)-C(H_q)$. Let $r > 0$ be
        minimal such that $y^r in C(H_q)$, let $l$ be a given prime factor of
        $r$, and let $z = y^(r slash l)$. Then $z^l in C(H_q)$, while
        $z in N(H_q)-C(H_q)$. Setting $H_q = chevron.l h chevron.r$, define
        $
          E = chevron.l h,z chevron.r slash chevron.l h^q,z^l chevron.r
          tilde.equiv C times.r chevron.l overline(z) chevron.r,
        $
        with $abs(C) = q$. Then $E$ is a factor group of a subgroup of $H$, so
        $E$ must be bad, which contradicts
        @lem:conlon-cyclic-prime-affine-good-group.

        We have now shown that for each prime divisor $q$ of $abs(H)$, $q != p$,
        $H$ contains a normal $q$-complement $N_q$. We set
        $ N = inter.big_q N_q ⊴ H. $
        Then obviously $N$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. Further, for each $q$
        as above, $H slash N$ has a cyclic Sylow $q$-subgroup and a normal
        $q$-complement. It follows that $H slash N$ is a direct product of its
        Sylow subgroups, so $H slash N$ is a cyclic $p'$-group. Therefore by the
        Schur–Zassenhaus Theorem, $H = N times.r T$ for some $T <= H$, with
        $T tilde.equiv H slash N$, and so $H$ is $p$-hypo-elementary. This
        completes the proof of the Conlon Induction Theorem. For another proof,
        see @th:conlon-p-permutation-induction.
      ] <case:conlon-bad-groups-hypo-elementary>],
  )
]

The field $K$ plays a subsidiary role in the preceding argument. The key
relations come from finite projective lattices, tensor induction and the
averaging and cyclotomic decompositions. This gives a stronger result.
#ed-note[The printed generalization uses the localization $ZZ_((p))$, but an
  allowed coefficient ring need not receive that localization. For example,
  $G=C_2$, $p=2$, $R=ZZ$ satisfies the hypotheses below, while $3$ is not
  invertible in $R$.]

For $R$ any commutative ring, let $a(R G)$ be the _representation ring_ of
$R G$, generated by $R G$-lattices with relations coming from direct sum (see
§~@ss:representation-ring-preliminaries). Set $A(R G) = QQ times.o_ZZ a(R G)$,
the _representation algebra_ of $R G$. Then we have:

#theorem[
  Given a group $G$ and a prime $p$, let $R$ be a commutative ring such that
  each prime divisor of $abs(G)$, except possibly $p$, is invertible in $R$.
  Then the Conlon Induction Theorem remains true when the field $K$ is replaced
  throughout by $R$.
] <th:conlon-induction-coefficient-ring>
#idx(
  "Conlon's Induction Theorem",
  target: [@th:conlon-induction-coefficient-ring],
)

#proof[
  Let $m$ be the product of the prime divisors of $abs(G)$ different from $p$,
  and put $R_0=ZZ[m^(-1)]$. The preceding argument works over $R_0$: the
  elementary-abelian relation follows from the averaging idempotents above; the
  cyclotomic decomposition requires only that the relevant prime $q$ be
  invertible. Tensor induction descends through the full lattice monoid and
  extends to the rational permutation span as in
  @case:conlon-inflation-tensor-norm. Thus the same bad-group argument produces
  the required unit relation over $R_0$. There is a canonical homomorphism
  $R_0 -> R$. Extending scalars preserves permutation modules, direct sums,
  tensor products and induction, and carries that unit relation to $A(R G)$. No
  homomorphism from $ZZ_((p))$ to an arbitrary such $R$ is needed.
]

Since the representation algebra $A(K G)$ is a Frobenius module over the
Frobenius functor $Omega_QQ lr((K G))$, we obtain the following consequence of
Conlon's Theorem:

#source(881)
#corollary[
  Let $K$ be a field of characteristic $p >= 0$, and let $cal(H)'$ denote the
  set of $p$-hypo-elementary subgroups of $G$. Then
  $ A(K G) = sum_(H in cal(H)') op("ind")_H^G A(K H). $
  Further, if $x in A(K G)$ is such that $op("res")_H^G x = 0$ for all
  $H in cal(H)'$, then $x = 0$. (These assertions remain valid when $K$ is
  replaced by a ring $R$ as in @th:conlon-induction-coefficient-ring.)
] <cor:conlon-representation-algebra-induction-detection>
#proof[
  The result is immediate from @cor:frobenius-induction-annihilators.
]

#exercises-heading

Throughout, $G$ is a finite group, $S,T$ are $G$-sets, and $H,K <= G$.

#exercise[
  Let $s in S$ and $H <= G$. Prove that for $x in G$, $s in op("inv")_H S$ if
  and only if $x s in op("inv")_(x H x^(-1)) S$. Deduce that
  $ abs(op("inv")_H S) = abs(op("inv")_K S) quad "whenever" H =_G K. $
] <exc:g-set-conjugate-fixed-point-marks>

#exercise[
  Let $H backslash G slash K$ be the collection of all $(H,K)$-double cosets
  $H g K$ of $G$. Show that each $G$-orbit of the left $G$-set
  $(G slash H) times
  (G slash K)$ determines a double coset $H g K$, by the rule:
  $ G"-orbit of" (x H,y K) |-> H dot x^(-1) y K. $
  Show that this correspondence gives a bijection between the collection of
  $G$-orbits of $(G slash H) times (G slash K)$ and the double coset space
  $H backslash G slash K$.
] <exc:coset-product-double-cosets>
#hint([@exc:coset-product-double-cosets])[
  Let $G = union.dot_a H a K$. The $G$-orbit of $(G slash H) times (G slash K)$
  corresponding to $H a K$ consists of all distinct pairs in the collection
  ${(x H,y K): x^(-1) y in H a K}$. The stabilizer of the pair $(H,a K)$ is
  precisely $H inter a K a^(-1)$. Deduce that the orbit of $(G slash H)
  times (G slash K)$ corresponding to $H a K$ is isomorphic (as $G$-set) to
  $G slash lr((H inter a K a^(-1)))$, and therefore
  $
    (G slash H) times (G slash K) tilde.equiv union.dot.big_a G slash lr(
      (H
        inter a K a^(-1))
    ).
  $
  Compare this with Mackey's Theorem @i:th:mackey-tensor-product.
] <hint:coset-product-double-cosets>

#exercise[
  Say that the group $G$ acts _freely_ on a $G$-set $S$ if for each $s in S$,
  its stabilizer $G_s$ equals $1$. Prove
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        $G$ acts freely on a $G$-set $S$ if and only if $S$ is a disjoint union
        of copies of the left $G$-set $G$.
      ] <case:free-g-set-regular-orbits>],
    [
      #case[If $G$ acts freely on $S$, then $abs(G)$ divides $abs(S)$.]
      <case:free-g-set-cardinality-divisibility>],
    [
      #case[
        If $G$ acts freely on $S$, and $H$ is a subgroup of $G$, then $H$ acts
        freely on $S$. Show further that $H$ acts freely on every $H$-subset of
        $S$, and deduce that $abs(H)$ divides the number of elements in any
        $H$-subset of $S$.
      ] <case:free-g-set-subgroup-invariant-subsets>],
  )
] <exc:free-g-sets-regular-orbits>

#exercise[
  For $H <= G$, let $N_G lr((H))$ act from the left as permutation group on the
  coset space $G slash H$,
  #source(882)
  by setting
  $ x dot g H = g x^(-1) H, quad x in N_G lr((H)), g H in G slash H. $
  Since the subgroup $H$ of $N_G lr((H))$ acts trivially on $G slash H$ under
  the above definition, it follows that $G slash H$ is a left $N_G lr((H))
  slash H$-set. Prove:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[$N_G lr((H)) slash H$ acts freely on $G slash H$.]
      <case:normalizer-quotient-free-coset-action>],
    [
      #case[
        For $K <= G$, let
        $
          op("inv")_K lr((G slash H)) & = {g H: x dot g H = g H "for all" x
                                          in K} \
                                      & = {g H: K dot g H = g H}.
        $
        Show that $op("inv")_K lr((G slash H))$ is an $N_G lr((H)) slash
        H$-subset of $G slash H$. Deduce that $N_G lr((H)) slash H$ acts freely
        on $op("inv")_K lr(
          (G
            slash H)
        )$.
      ] <case:normalizer-quotient-free-fixed-point-action>],
    [
      #case[
        Keeping the above notation, prove that
        $ abs(N_G lr((H)):H) "divides" abs(op("inv")_K lr((G slash H))) $
        for each subgroup $K$ of $G$.
      ] <case:normalizer-divides-fixed-point-marks>],
  )
] <exc:normalizer-divides-fixed-point-marks>

#exercise[
  Keeping the notation of the preceding exercise, prove that
  $ N_G lr((H)) slash H tilde.equiv Hom_G lr((G slash H,G slash H)) $
  as permutation groups on $G slash H$.
] <exc:coset-g-set-automorphism-normalizer>
#hint([@exc:coset-g-set-automorphism-normalizer])[
  For $x in N_G lr((H))$, let $theta_x: G slash H -> G slash H$ be the $G$-map
  defined by $theta_x lr((g H)) = g x^(-1) H$ for $g H in G slash H$. Then
  $theta: x H |-> theta_x$, $x in N_G lr((H))$, gives the desired isomorphism.
] <hint:coset-g-set-automorphism-normalizer>

#exercise[
  Prove that if $G$ acts freely on $S$, it also acts freely on $S times T$ for
  each $T$.
] <exc:free-g-set-cartesian-product>

#exercise[
  Let $abs(G) = p = "prime"$. Find all simple $G$-sets; compute $Omega(G)$ and
  its image under the monomorphism $phi$ defined in
  @prop:burnside-mark-full-lattice. Determine all prime ideals of $Omega(G)$,
  and also all units in $Omega(G)$. Answer the same questions for the case
  $G = S_3$.
] <exc:cyclic-prime-symmetric-three-burnside-rings>

#exercise[
  For $H,K <= G$, let $(G slash H)_K$ be the $K$-set obtained by restriction of
  operators on the $G$-set $G slash H$. Show that each $K$-orbit of $(G
    slash H)_K$ is precisely the collection of all left cosets $g H$ in some
  $(K,H)$-double coset of $G$. If $G = union.dot_g K g H$, then each $g$
  determines a $K$-orbit of $(G slash H)_K$. Prove that
  $
    K g H tilde.equiv K slash lr((K inter attach(H, tl: g))) quad "as left"
    K"-sets".
  $
] <exc:restricted-coset-g-set-orbits>

#exercise[
  Let $H <= G$. Show there exists an $x in Omega(G)$ with
  $ phi_H lr((x)) != 0, quad phi_K lr((x)) = 0 quad "for" K !=_G H. $
] <exc:burnside-single-conjugacy-mark-support>
#hint([@exc:burnside-single-conjugacy-mark-support])[
  Using the notation in @eq:burnside-total-mark-homomorphism and
  @prop:burnside-mark-full-lattice, we may assume $H$ is some $H_i$. Let
  $epsilon_i$ be the element of $ZZ^((m))$ that has $i$-th component $1$, all
  others $0$. By @cor:burnside-mark-cokernel-exponent,
  $abs(G) epsilon_i = phi(x)$ for some $x in Omega(G)$. For this $x$ we have
  $phi_H lr((x)) = abs(G)$, $phi_K lr((x)) = 0$ if $K !=_G H$.
] <hint:burnside-single-conjugacy-mark-support>

#source(883)
#exercise[
  Each group homomorphism $tau: H -> G$ induces an additive map
  $tau^ast: Omega(G) -> Omega(H)$, by viewing each $G$-set as an $H$-set via
  $tau$. Prove that $tau$ is surjective if and only if $tau^ast$ is injective.
] <exc:burnside-restriction-injective-surjective-group-map>
#hint([@exc:burnside-restriction-injective-surjective-group-map])[
  If $tau$ is surjective, then two $G$-sets are $G$-isomorphic if and only if
  they are $tau(H)$-isomorphic, that is, $H$-isomorphic. Thus $tau^ast$ is
  injective in this case. Conversely, assume $tau^ast$ injective. By Exercise
  @exc:burnside-single-conjugacy-mark-support, we may choose $x in Omega(G)$
  such that $phi_G lr((x)) != 0$, $phi_K lr((x)) = 0$ for $K < G$. Then the
  restriction $x_H$ (via $tau$) is nonzero in $Omega(H)$, so
  $phi_E lr((x_H)) != 0$ for some $E <= H$. But
  $ phi_E lr((x_H)) = abs(op("inv")_E x_H) = abs(op("inv")_(tau(E)) x), $
  so $abs(op("inv")_(tau(E)) x) != 0$. Therefore $tau(E) = G$, so $tau$ is
  surjective.
] <hint:burnside-restriction-injective-surjective-group-map>

#exercise[
  Let $S,T$ be left $G$-sets, and define
  $
    S^T = {f: T -> S}, quad "with" (x f)t = x f(x^(-1) t), quad x in G, t in T.
  $
  Then $S^T$ is a left $G$-set. Prove:
  #case-list(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [
      #case[
        The map $psi: Omega^+(G) -> Omega(G)$, defined by $psi[S] = [S^T]$, is
        multiplicative, and is algebraic of degree $<= abs(T)$.
      ] <case:g-set-exponentiation-algebraic-map>],
    [
      #case[
        $psi$ extends uniquely to an algebraic multiplicative map
        $psi': Omega(G) -> Omega(G)$. For $x in Omega(G)$, denote $psi'(x)$ by
        $x^T$. Then:
      ] <case:g-set-exponentiation-extension>],
    [
      #case[
        For $x,y in Omega(G)$ and for $G$-sets $T,U$, we have
        $ (x y)^T = x^T y^T, quad x^(T union.dot U) = x^T dot x^U. $
      ] <case:g-set-exponentiation-product-disjoint-union>],
    [
      #case[
        For $H <= G$, $x in Omega(G)$,
        $
          phi_G lr((x^(G slash H))) = abs(op("inv")_G x^(G slash H)) =
          abs(op("inv")_H x)
          = phi_H lr((x)),
        $
        where $phi_G,phi_H$ are defined as in @eq:burnside-fixed-point-mark.
      ] <case:g-set-exponentiation-mark-adjunction>],
  )
] <exc:g-set-exponentiation-algebraic-extension>
#hint([@exc:g-set-exponentiation-algebraic-extension])[
  See Dress–Küchler~@bib:Dress1970a, p.~82 for details.
] <hint:g-set-exponentiation-algebraic-extension>

#exercise[
  Let $S$ be an $H$-set, where $H <= G$, and let $phi_H,phi_G$ be as in
  @eq:burnside-fixed-point-mark. Show that
  $phi_(H)[S] = phi_(G)[S^(times.o G)]$.
] <exc:tensor-induction-fixed-point-adjunction>
#hint([@exc:tensor-induction-fixed-point-adjunction])[
  Use @th:g-set-tensor-induction-right-adjunction with $S = e_G$.
] <hint:tensor-induction-fixed-point-adjunction>

#exercise[
  Show that there is a homomorphism $Omega(G) -> op("ch")_QQ G$, given by
  mapping each $G$-set $S$ onto the character of $G$ afforded by the permutation
  module $QQ[S]$. Prove that there is a surjection of $QQ$-algebras
  $ QQ times.o_ZZ Omega(G) -> QQ times.o_ZZ op("ch")_QQ G, $
  and show by example that the map need not be injective.
] <exc:rational-burnside-character-surjection>

#exercise[
  Let $H <= G$, $abs(G : H) = n$, and let $S,T$ be $H$-sets. Prove that
  $
    (S union.dot T)^(times.o G) tilde.equiv S^(times.o G) union.dot
    T^(times.o G)
    union.dot (union.dot.big X_i),
  $
  with each $X_i$ a $G$-set induced from some proper subgroup of $G$.
] <exc:tensor-induction-mixed-subsets-induced>
#source(884)
#hint([@exc:tensor-induction-mixed-subsets-induced])[
  Define $V_k$ as in @cor:tensor-induction-alternating-colored-subsets, and show
  that for $1 <= k <= n-1$, the $G$-stabilizer of each $v in V_k$ is a proper
  subgroup of $G$.
] <hint:tensor-induction-mixed-subsets-induced>

#exercise[
  Let $E < H < G$, and let $S$ be an $E$-set. Prove that
  $ (op("ind")_E^H S)^(times.o G) $
  is disjoint union of $G$-sets induced from proper subgroups of $G$.
] <exc:tensor-induction-proper-induced-set>

#exercise[
  Show that subgroups and factor groups of $p$-hypo-elementary groups are
  $p$-hypo-elementary.
] <exc:hypo-elementary-subgroups-quotients>

#exercise[
  Show that every $G$-functor $F$ is a module over the Burnside functor $Omega$.
] <exc:g-functor-burnside-module>
#hint([@exc:g-functor-burnside-module])[
  For $H <= G$, let $Omega(H)$ act on $F(H)$ thus:
  $ [H slash E] dot x = (e_E)^H x = (x_E)^H, $
  where $E <= H$ and $x in F(H)$. Then check Frobenius reciprocity.
] <hint:g-functor-burnside-module>
